import{_ as a,P as s,d as r,e as o,x as t,q as n}from"./chunks/framework.BRr8VP3a.js";const g=JSON.parse(`{"title":"Part 13 Interfaces and Tools","description":"","frontmatter":{"title":"Part 13 Interfaces and Tools","xkChars":0,"xkWords":4769,"xkLessons":{"intro-13-part":{"title":"Part XIII: Interfaces and Tools","lead":"Tools make judgments' grounds and boundaries visible; they add neither facts to the world nor powers to their users.","explanation":["Chinese source location: lines 2725–2734; original-site location: Part XIII introduction, without an independently numbered anchor. See the theory-v2 source for the current full text; the historical research manuscript and excerpts remain downloadable in their original versions.","The introduction connects objects, propositions, scales, mechanisms, actors, joint states, and dynamic projection to bounded judgments that are observable, challengeable, reviewable, and retractable. Its key phrase is, in translation, “It adds no new facts about the world.” Tools may detect inconsistent records, organize existing grounds, or propose candidates, but complete formatting is not evidence that an empirical claim holds. [T-intro]","Before entering the formal tool flow, a D0 candidate-object record and nine-axis scale record are required. This does not declare complete empirical support for the object. It first fixes the object, boundaries, time window, identity, and use under discussion, so later evidence has a clear claim to support. When object, scale, or use changes, existing judgments cannot simply transfer; reexamine from the changed dependency. [T-intro]","In the two-level structure, the seven gates are the sole main process, deciding whether judgment may enter the next level; the five-gate thirteen-step scheme only expands material needed for complex tasks. It cannot create a separate “passed” status overriding failure in the seven gates. This permits complex evidence gathering while preventing more forms from producing contradictory final statuses. [T-intro; T-seven; T-expand]","Passing every tool gate still yields only descriptions, candidate mechanisms, comparative judgments, strong-judgment candidates, or action requirements. Actual actions additionally require structured choice, C12's ten-component bridge, one valid atomic J authorization, and O1–O4. These are external dependencies, not extra internal stamps allowing tools to authorize themselves. Part XIII's central separation is between judgment ceilings and authority to act. [T-intro; T-record]","Reading, source-text proofreading, and low-impact clarification may use proportionate concise records; running every procedure is not a prerequisite for recognizing established domain facts. Formal diagnosis, foresight, or high-impact use requires complete contracts. Renaming the same high-impact use “reading notes” cannot reduce its obligations. Actual use, not length, distinguishes low-impact simplification from high-impact circumvention. [T-intro]","This introduction primarily specifies the chapter's architecture and boundaries; it makes no empirical claim requiring a particular historical event as proof. Accordingly, this standalone unit does not force in a historical case. The next numbered section uses checked original research to explain limits on transitions among measurement, evidence, and judgment. The flowchart here also only maps relationships in the source text; it does not claim an actual improvement in judgment accuracy. [T-intro]"],"argumentSteps":["Identify the actual task and use, distinguishing concise reading from formal diagnosis.","With a D0 candidate object and nine-axis scale record in place, fix the version and boundaries.","Use the seven gates to manage status uniformly; expand complex material through the five gates and thirteen steps.","Outputs reach only the corresponding judgment or action requirements; execution permissions are checked separately through external contracts.","Review again when the object or use changes; do not retain the original high-impact use by renaming it."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Tool completion","leftText":"No gaps remain in the prescribed recording gates.","rightTitle":"The fact holds","rightText":"Requires domain proposition contracts and evidence."},{"leftTitle":"Diagnostic expansion layer","leftText":"Develops material in detail.","rightTitle":"Main-process status","rightText":"Managed only by the seven gates; two competing systems are not allowed."},{"leftTitle":"Action requirements","leftText":"Lists required conditions and prohibited items.","rightTitle":"Authorization to act","rightText":"Valid external authorization and execution conditions are not generated by the tool."}],"boundaries":["Source-text boundary: low-impact proofreading may use concise records; not running every tool does not deny established domain facts.","Source-text boundary: formal high-impact use cannot bypass complete contracts by renaming the output.","Teaching counterexample: filling every box in a form with “checked,” without sources or permissions, cannot raise factual or action ceilings."],"questions":["If the seven gates fail, can the five gates and thirteen steps issue a separate pass?","Can completing all tools substitute for someone else's consent?","Must low-impact source proofreading run all tools before acknowledging that a quotation exists?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-13-1/figures/architecture.png","caseLinks":[],"caseNotes":[],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"intro-13-part","start_line":2805,"end_line":2814,"startLine":2805,"endLine":2814}],"outputId":"intro-13-part","sourceHash":"51795bb7988f2669a324a52666c320cfba1382f597f7b5c6a1431844572da1fb","teachingExamples":[{"title":"Teaching construction: moving from proofreading to personnel use requires reopening","text":"Constructed request A checks only whether a passage faithfully quotes a supplied text; source locations, short quotations, and discrepancies can suffice. Request B uses the same appraisal to decide whether someone retains a position, adding obligations concerning impact, protection against retaliation, appeals, strong judgments, and authorization. A's completed proofreading status cannot be copied into B as grounds for action.","assumptions":"Entirely constructed; no specific people or actual personnel operations are involved.","boundary":"This is not legal advice about actual employment relationships; it only explains how changing use changes evidential obligations."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Source-text boundary: low-impact proofreading may use concise records; not running every tool does not deny established domain facts.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-intro"]},{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Source-text boundary: formal high-impact use cannot bypass complete contracts by renaming the output.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-intro"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Teaching counterexample: filling every box in a form with “checked,” without sources or permissions, cannot raise factual or action ceilings.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-intro"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"If the seven gates fail, can the five gates and thirteen steps issue a separate pass?","answer":"No. The expansion layer has no competing main-process status.","basis":"Two-level structure."},{"question":"Can completing all tools substitute for someone else's consent?","answer":"No. Tools generate no authorization and do not replace participants' own expression.","basis":"The introduction's authorization boundary."},{"question":"Must low-impact source proofreading run all tools before acknowledging that a quotation exists?","answer":"No. Use records proportionate to the task; review high-impact use separately.","basis":"The introduction's final paragraph."}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-13-1/figures/architecture.png","alt":"D0 and nine-axis entry; seven-gate main process; five-gate, thirteen-step expansion; independent real-world authorization.","caption":"A diagram of theoretical structure, not proof that software has passed."}],"diagramBrief":"A static hierarchy diagram: entry records→the sole main process, with diagnostic expansion alongside and authorized execution outside the tools. Future interactive design would only display required records by use, without changing permission status.","applicability":{"realCase":"not_applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"This unit guides the chapter's process and methodological boundaries. It does not use irrelevant historical events to certify a normative architecture; the constructed change-of-use example only explains the boundary."},"openQuestions":["No reader study or process-effectiveness experiment was conducted; the architecture cannot be claimed to have demonstrated efficacy."],"taskId":"S-13-1","sources":[{"id":"T-intro","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§intro-13-part, Chinese source lines 2671–2680","claim_support":["Tool-layer obligations, two-level structure, authorization separation, and low-impact simplification."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"T-main","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§13.1, Chinese source lines 2681–2695","claim_support":["IT1–IT4 requirements, ceilings on failure, and dependency/global/independent-chain rules."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"T-seven","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§13.3, Chinese source lines 2702–2719","claim_support":["The seven gates as the sole main process."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"T-expand","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§13.4, Chinese source lines 2720–2751","claim_support":["The five gates and thirteen steps as an expansion layer."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"T-record","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§13.9, Chinese source lines 2802–2813","claim_support":["Action requirements are not execution commands; authorization_effect is always none."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"P-CC","title":"Carlson et al., Effect of Consumer Direction on Adults’ Personal Care and Well-Being in Arkansas, New Jersey and Florida","url":"https://aspe.hhs.gov/sites/default/files/migrated_legacy_files/41521/adultpcw.pdf","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"2005-05-16","locator":"2005-05-16, printed pages 12–14 and 48; PDF pages 24–26 and 60.","claim_support":["The original research distinguishes random access to a program opportunity from actual receipt; constructs endpoints from survey records; and states proxy exclusions, self-reporting, and short-term limitations."],"limits":["The study design does not mean that the four conversions were each implemented in reality. This lesson uses its measurement and inference boundaries only to explain IT1–IT3, without making individual service decisions."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"theory-v2","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v2, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v2 / 2026-11-16","locator":"Part Thirteen: Interfaces and Tools; Chinese source lines 2725–2734; SHA-256 6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v2; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-13-1/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-13-1/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-13-1/source/S-13-1.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-13-1/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text and section locations correspond to v2; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"be05b5e3d1cf9a15ee8668a67bb9fea1a9c33472bbe15f36c62a635f28ed1e20","source_sha256":"6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c"}},"13.1":{"title":"13.1 Four conversions","lead":"Moving from “having a concept” to “how strong a judgment is justified” involves four different evidential conversions. Even the final step only lists action conditions; it does not turn a judgment into a command.","explanation":["Source location: Chinese source lines 2735–2749; original site location /theory/part-13#section-13-1. See the theory-v2 source for the current full text; the historical research original and excerpts remain available for download and checking in their original versions.","The source specifies that the four conversions “successively add recording obligations” (translated from Chinese). Structural variables, signals, evidence, judgments, and action requirements are not synonyms: each forward step requires additional support. Downstream formatting cannot remedy failure. At the same time, genuinely independent judgments that do not rely on the failed item may retain their own grounds, avoiding the exaggeration of a local failure into the disappearance of all knowledge. [T-main]","IT1 maps structural variables to observable signals. It must specify the object, scale, observation position, time window, measurement protocol, and missingness status. For example, “declining trust” cannot directly count as data: specify who left a record, when, and through which questions or behavioral channels. If IT1 is insufficient, stop at conceptual mapping or a material gap. A self-designed proxy indicator does not mean real-world measurement is complete. [T-main]","IT2 moves from signals to evidence strength, requiring separate treatment of sources, observations, comparison conditions, coverage, counterexamples, alternative explanations, and uncertainty. A source's existence only makes material traceable; it does not automatically support the stated conclusion. Numerous repetitions from one source do not become independent comparisons. A reliable signal may still fail to discriminate for the current proposition. Insufficient material remains ungraded material or a hypothesis awaiting testing. [T-main]","IT3 converts evidence into a judgment level, requiring a proposition contract, applicability boundaries, contrary conditions, and conditions for falsification and withdrawal. Correlations, analogies, or a single mechanistic story cannot automatically become causal judgments. Changing the proposition may change its evidential obligations. Support for a group comparison over nine months does not mean equal support for every individual or long-term effects. [T-main]","IT4 converts judgment levels into action requirements and ceilings, listing affected positions, the protection floor, prohibited actions, stopping, appeals, rollback, and missing N/J/O/C12 items. The output may be a descriptive requirement, a requirement for further evidence, or an inaction requirement, not an executable command. Even if all four conversions pass, authorization status remains entirely unchanged: they only state what external conditions are needed and cannot sign off those conditions themselves. [T-main；T-record]","Failure propagates along dependencies. If IT2 has run and failed, dependent IT3/IT4 can only be not_run. Not run cannot be recorded as not applicable; opening another branch, renaming, or completing all eight SJ items cannot restore the ceiling. If failure affects global source identity, shared factual premises, object boundaries, or lawful use of the entire task, all dependent outputs stop together. Preserved independent judgments must have genuinely independent premises and compliant uses, rather than merely copying the same gap. [T-main]","Cash and Counseling provides a bounded comparison for the conversions: the study measures service and experience endpoints, distinguishes randomly receiving a program opportunity from actual receipt, and retains self-reporting, proxy-exclusion, and short-term limitations. This helps explain why a survey value requires comparison and scope constraints before becoming a conclusion. An opportunity effect cannot be rewritten as an unconditional individual effect on recipients, nor may the study automatically determine anyone's service arrangements. This lesson does not claim that the research team used IT numbering. [P-CC]","The strongest objection is that four conversion layers obstruct clear statements. Their aim is not to make all conclusions vague: bounded propositions supported by independent material can be stated clearly, while failed dependency chains must clearly stop. Explicitly stating “which link is unknown and which conclusion does not follow” is more reviewable than disguising certainty through complete formatting. [T-main]"],"argumentSteps":["Map structural variables to actual signals with protocols and windows: IT1.","Check signals' comparative, coverage, and counterexample support for the specific proposition: IT2.","Use proposition contracts to bound judgment levels, falsification, and withdrawal: IT3.","List protection, prohibitions, stopping, rollback, and missing external conditions: IT4; do not issue authorization.","If any step fails, mark dependent steps not_run and lower the ceiling; handle independent chains and global gaps separately."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Signal record","leftText":"A channel leaves locatable material.","rightTitle":"Evidence strength","rightText":"How strongly material supports a specific proposition, and its limitations."},{"leftTitle":"failed","leftText":"Was run and did not satisfy the requirements.","rightTitle":"not_run","rightText":"Was not run because of dependencies or scope; cannot be disguised as success."},{"leftTitle":"Independent judgment","leftText":"Does not depend on a failed premise and has a compliant use.","rightTitle":"Renamed branch","rightText":"Still depends on the same gap and cannot restore the ceiling."}],"boundaries":["Source-text boundary: passing all four conversions does not change authorization status.","Source-text boundary: failure of global sources or shared premises blocks every dependent output.","Teaching counterexample: if temperature records are independently checkable but identity-data provenance fails, the former may remain for their narrow use; this does not permit continuation of eligibility judgments dependent on identity."],"questions":["After IT2 fails, can IT3 pass because its template is complete?","Does fully traceable provenance automatically complete IT2?","If all four pass, may the actions listed in IT4 be executed?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-13-1/figures/transforms.png","caseLinks":[{"title":"Research endpoints cannot bypass comparison and use limitations","href":"https://aspe.hhs.gov/sites/default/files/migrated_legacy_files/41521/adultpcw.pdf"}],"caseNotes":[{"title":"Research endpoints cannot bypass comparison and use limitations","text":"The 2005 Cash and Counseling report estimates the effect of randomly obtaining a program opportunity, rather than regrouping actual recipients and still calling it a randomized comparison. Endpoints come from surveys, and the report retains proxy-exclusion and short-term limitations.","sourceLabel":"Carlson et al., Effect of Consumer Direction on Adults’ Personal Care and Well-Being in Arkansas, New Jersey and Florida","sourceUrl":"https://aspe.hhs.gov/sites/default/files/migrated_legacy_files/41521/adultpcw.pdf","sourceLocator":"2005-05-16, printed pages 12–14 and 48; PDF pages 24–26 and 60.","boundary":"Used to explain obligations from signals to bounded judgments. It does not claim these methods eliminated all bias and makes no individual service or eligibility decisions.","href":"https://aspe.hhs.gov/sites/default/files/migrated_legacy_files/41521/adultpcw.pdf"}],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"13.1","start_line":2815,"end_line":2829,"startLine":2815,"endLine":2829}],"outputId":"section-13-1","sourceHash":"51795bb7988f2669a324a52666c320cfba1382f597f7b5c6a1431844572da1fb","teachingExamples":[{"title":"Teaching construction: a local broken chain and an independent branch","text":"A constructed task includes proposition A, “the equipment temperature was 20 degrees at 9 today,” from a checked instrument protocol, and proposition B, “an employee deliberately caused the fault,” based only on an unchecked anonymous screenshot. Suppose A's measurement and provenance support its bounded description, while B fails IT2 comparison and identity tracing. B's IT3/IT4 are then not_run; adding a complete appeal form cannot turn B into a strong conclusion. A does not depend on the screenshot and may remain. If the instrument number later proves to identify the wrong object, A must also be reviewed from the earliest affected point.","assumptions":"All objects, material, and numbers are constructed; no actual employee is accused.","boundary":"No formal attribution of responsibility was performed. A supports only the specified temperature description, not the cause of the fault."},{"title":"Teaching construction: output requirements are not execution permission","text":"A constructed check finds two conflicting versions of a record and may propose the conditional requirement “do not use this for eligibility decisions before independent review, and preserve an entry point for additional evidence.” This does not authorize researchers to delete records or change anyone's eligibility. The requirement points to external procedures; actual actions still need corresponding permissions.","assumptions":"The records and conflicts are entirely constructed; no actual deletion or eligibility action occurred.","boundary":"Conditional requirements and executable commands are separate in use and permission."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Source-text boundary: passing all four conversions does not change authorization status.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Source-text boundary: failure of global sources or shared premises blocks every dependent output.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Teaching counterexample: if temperature records are independently checkable but identity-data provenance fails, the former may remain for their narrow use; this does not permit continuation of eligibility judgments dependent on identity.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"After IT2 fails, can IT3 pass because its template is complete?","answer":"No. Steps dependent on IT2 can only remain not_run and retain the downgrade.","basis":"Failure dependency rule."},{"question":"Does fully traceable provenance automatically complete IT2?","answer":"No. The proposition's observations, comparisons, coverage, counterexamples, and uncertainty still require examination.","basis":"Evidence is not merely provenance."},{"question":"If all four pass, may the actions listed in IT4 be executed?","answer":"No. IT4 lists conditions and ceilings; authorization status is unchanged.","basis":"This section's authorization separation."}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-13-1/figures/transforms.png","alt":"IT1 signals, IT2 evidence, IT3 judgments, and IT4 requirements differ at each layer; the endpoint still grants no authorization.","caption":"Based on the original table in §13.1: not run is not passed."},{"path":"/reading-assets/S-13-1/figures/failure.png","alt":"After IT2 fails, IT3/IT4 are not_run. Independent branches can be reviewed separately; global gaps affect all dependencies.","caption":"A constructed dependency diagram; it does not indicate that any actual diagnosis is complete."}],"diagramBrief":"Two static diagrams show the four conversions and the dependency-failure chain. Future interaction could select a failure point to display blocked downstream steps while preserving genuinely independent branches; it cannot restore permissions with one click.","applicability":{"realCase":"applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"This unit concerns methodological judgment. Actual sources support only the listed facts; the diagram and teaching construction each retain their own status."},"openQuestions":["No actual high-impact diagnosis or authorization process was run; the constructed failure chain does not represent a real system's status."],"taskId":"S-13-1","sources":[{"id":"T-intro","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§intro-13-part, Chinese source lines 2671–2680","claim_support":["Tool-layer obligations, two-level structure, authorization separation, and low-impact simplification."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"T-main","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§13.1, Chinese source lines 2681–2695","claim_support":["IT1–IT4 requirements, ceilings on failure, and dependency/global/independent-chain rules."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"T-seven","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§13.3, Chinese source lines 2702–2719","claim_support":["The seven gates as the sole main process."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"T-expand","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§13.4, Chinese source lines 2720–2751","claim_support":["The five gates and thirteen steps as an expansion layer."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"T-record","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§13.9, Chinese source lines 2802–2813","claim_support":["Action requirements are not execution commands; authorization_effect is always none."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"P-CC","title":"Carlson et al., Effect of Consumer Direction on Adults’ Personal Care and Well-Being in Arkansas, New Jersey and Florida","url":"https://aspe.hhs.gov/sites/default/files/migrated_legacy_files/41521/adultpcw.pdf","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"2005-05-16","locator":"2005-05-16, printed pages 12–14 and 48; PDF pages 24–26 and 60.","claim_support":["The original research distinguishes random access to a program opportunity from actual receipt; constructs endpoints from survey records; and states proxy exclusions, self-reporting, and short-term limitations."],"limits":["The study design does not mean that the four conversions were each implemented in reality. This lesson uses its measurement and inference boundaries only to explain IT1–IT3, without making individual service decisions."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"theory-v2","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v2, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v2 / 2026-11-16","locator":"13.1 Four conversions; Chinese source lines 2735–2749; SHA-256 6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v2; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-13-1/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-13-1/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-13-1/source/S-13-1.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-13-1/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text and section locations correspond to v2; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"d2a40ae6f6897bfeb18240bc64bc9ab0d45ccd3f0d9b145e3e58d0ab49249f89","source_sha256":"6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c"}},"13.2":{"title":"13.2 Using the universal primitives and H-World variable interfaces (11+11)","lead":"The two sets of eleven interfaces can be used together, but they are not a dictionary whose columns translate one-to-one. Choosing an interface also requires clarity about the kind of conclusion being proposed.","explanation":["Source location: Chinese source lines 2750–2755; original site location /theory/part-13#section-13-2. See the theory-v2 source for the current full text; the historical research original and excerpts remain available for download and checking in their original versions.","The source places universal primitives U01–U11 alongside H-World interfaces HV01–HV11 and explicitly states that matching numbers, quantities, or questions do not imply one-to-one equivalence. Universal primitives provide descriptive positions for candidate objects, boundaries, states, components, constraints, transmission, feedback, environment, load, traces, and phases. Human interfaces additionally introduce specific obligations concerning meaning, subjects/carriers/responsibility, power, and voluntary assumption of burdens. Properties of physical carriers cannot be directly transferred into human willingness or obligations. [T-main；T-U；T-HV]","Same-number counterexamples make the issue clearest: U03 is states and observables, while HV03 is orientation anchors; U04 is components, carriers, and substrates, while HV04 is generative nodes. Both U07 and HV07 concern return, but the former requires no institutional write-back, while the latter must distinguish acceptance, field changes, and actual execution. Similarity can generate questions; formal dependence must return to interface routing rather than connecting by number alone. [T-U；T-HV]","Each invocation records at least the interface ID, valid object, nine-axis scale, support route, instance evidence, alternative explanations, missingness status, and judgment and action ceilings. IDs prevent mixing identically named items; objects and scales bound applicability; routes determine required evidence; instances and alternatives bound support; missingness and ceilings make unperformed work visible. This does not require filling in all private information. Route-irrelevant items may be marked not applicable with reasons; retain key unknowns rather than invent values. [T-main；T-fields]","The support route matters more than the interface name. HV02's basic boundary list can be recorded when object material exists, without rigidly requiring G2; a selective-channel-effect claim requires the relevant G2-instance and causal gate. HV03 candidate meaning material does not first require H1 to hold; the effective-anchor route requires an H1-instance. Insufficient evidence for a higher tier must not block lower-tier facts, while a complete basic card cannot smuggle in a higher-tier mechanism. [T-HV]","H1, H4, and H5 enter designated empirical routes through their respective H-instances, with one prescribed subtype and success criterion preselected before the outcome. H5 additionally requires one specific carrier atomic reference and family consistency. H2 and H3 are typological rules; H6 is a normative boundary. They cannot substitute for empirical support. G1–G4 also require corresponding G-instances frozen before outcomes. Failure of positive support defaults to unsupported or unresolved, not automatic support for a null model. Adding an after-the-fact form to an existing historical report does not turn it into a preregistered instance. [T-main；T-Hinst；T-Hroles；T-Ginst]","“Only through instances” bounds qualifications for empirical upgrades within this framework; it does not deny facts supported by other domain methods. Task arrangements and data support in NASA reports can form candidate object, role, and channel lists. Additional claims that coordination improved efficiency, left persistent traces, or determined responsibility require the corresponding mechanisms, windows, and grounds for responsibility separately. Historical material is not presented here as a G/H instance registered before the outcome. [P-NASA；T-main]","The strongest objection is that two numbering systems add burden. Within an explicit task, invoke only necessary interfaces and separate formal references from reader-facing prose: the prose explains the question, evidence, and boundaries, while records retain precise IDs, routes, and versions. Simplification must not delete dependencies that change a conclusion's qualifications, nor should every ordinary reading task be forced to create twenty-two forms. [T-main；T-fields]"],"argumentSteps":["First state the real-world question and proposed conclusion, then select U or HV interfaces.","Check applicable objects and nine-axis scales; do not align automatically by matching numbers.","Select support routes and distinguish basic description, classification, conditional effects, and cross-period explanation.","Routes requiring G/H instances accept only instances meeting pre-outcome contracts; do not disguise old material after the fact.","Retain alternative explanations, missingness status, and output ceilings item by item; permissions remain externally checked."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"U primitives","leftText":"Interfaces for universal structural description and candidate questions.","rightTitle":"HV interfaces","rightText":"Interfaces for specific questions and obligations in H-World."},{"leftTitle":"Basic route","leftText":"Can first record supported facts and gaps.","rightTitle":"Empirical upgrade route","rightText":"Requires the G/H instances and corresponding evidence needed for the conclusion."},{"leftTitle":"H2/H3/H6","leftText":"Typological/classificatory/normative constraints.","rightTitle":"H1/H4/H5","rightText":"Families of empirical hypotheses requiring concrete instance tests."}],"boundaries":["Source-text boundary: similarity in the two sets' counts, numbering, or questions does not establish one-to-one equivalence.","Teaching counterexample: a machine's U11 phase change does not give it HV10 qualifications for human intentional collective S0–S6.","Source-text boundary: a fully filled interface card cannot prove object facts; abstract H/G names have no instance truth value."],"questions":["U09 and HV09 both discuss load. Can they be exchanged unconditionally?","If H1 has not passed, does that mean even participants' expressions cannot be recorded?","Can a preregistration form written after reading a historical report make its G-instance qualified?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-13-2/figures/two-sets.png","caseLinks":[{"title":"Historical documents first become lists, not automatically qualified instances","href":"https://ntrs.nasa.gov/api/citations/19700076776/downloads/19700076776.pdf"}],"caseNotes":[{"title":"Historical documents first become lists, not automatically qualified instances","text":"The Apollo 13 report records the Board's task and arrangements for MSC team data, analysis, and coordination, supporting role and channel facts; further causal and cross-period claims need separate checks.","sourceLabel":"Report of Apollo 13 Review Board","sourceUrl":"https://ntrs.nasa.gov/api/citations/19700076776/downloads/19700076776.pdf","sourceLocator":"June 1970, printed pp.1-1–1-3,2-1–2-2; PDF pp.14–16,32–33.","boundary":"Historical material supports only bounded description here; no framework preregistration is fabricated for 1970 material, and execution carriers are not equated with responsible parties.","href":"https://ntrs.nasa.gov/api/citations/19700076776/downloads/19700076776.pdf"}],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"13.2","start_line":2830,"end_line":2835,"startLine":2830,"endLine":2835}],"outputId":"section-13-2","sourceHash":"51795bb7988f2669a324a52666c320cfba1382f597f7b5c6a1431844572da1fb","teachingExamples":[{"title":"Teaching construction: one interface, two routes","text":"An artificial access log records A admitted and B refused on a particular day, preserving rules and times. HV02's basic route may register boundaries and access inventories, but “access control reduced B's income” is a separate causal claim requiring comparisons, alternatives, actual transmission, and the corresponding instance. Without a pre-outcome instance, the inventory cannot become G2 support; this also does not delete an observed refusal.","assumptions":"People, logs, and events are artificial, not real exclusion or income data.","boundary":"Separate observed facts from causal effects; recording refusal certifies neither the rule's justification nor authorization to act against someone."},{"title":"Teaching construction: U03 is not HV03","text":"An artificial thermometer records 20 degrees indoors; U03 can describe observation position, error, and time. This is not meaning-related material about goals, identity, or commitment, nor does number 03 turn it into an HV03 orientation anchor. A meeting slogan would at most be candidate meaning-related material, not evidence of common will.","assumptions":"Entirely artificial, with no actual physical or group measurement.","boundary":"Shows that numbering cannot supply a mapping, without denying joint use for suitable questions."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Source-text boundary: similarity in the two sets' counts, numbering, or questions does not establish one-to-one equivalence.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Teaching counterexample: a machine's U11 phase change does not give it HV10 qualifications for human intentional collective S0–S6.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Source-text boundary: a fully filled interface card cannot prove object facts; abstract H/G names have no instance truth value.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"U09 and HV09 both discuss load. Can they be exchanged unconditionally?","answer":"No. Human tasks, hidden labor, voluntariness, responsibility, and so forth require actual HV routes, with matching units and windows.","basis":"The two interface sets have different scopes."},{"question":"If H1 has not passed, does that mean even participants' expressions cannot be recorded?","answer":"No. Candidate meaning-related material may remain, but not a supported effective anchor.","basis":"HV03 route levels."},{"question":"Can a preregistration form written after reading a historical report make its G-instance qualified?","answer":"No. Retrospective forms cannot restore prior freezing conditions; the material can remain exploratory or candidate.","basis":"Instance contract."}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-13-2/figures/two-sets.png","alt":"U03 state observation contrasts with HV03 meaning anchors; U04 carriers contrast with HV04 generative nodes; no same-number equivalence arrows.","caption":"Based on §3.5 and §7.3; only two explicit counterexamples, not an exhaustive mapping."},{"path":"/reading-assets/S-13-2/figures/routes.png","alt":"Basic records and effect routes need different material, with G/H instances and authorization boundaries separate.","caption":"Based on §13.2; retrospective form completion does not satisfy a pre-outcome contract."}],"diagramBrief":"Two static figures show nonequivalence of equal numbers and increasing route requirements. A future basic/effect switch may display added evidence needs without automatically upgrading instance status.","applicability":{"realCase":"applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"This unit concerns methodological judgment. Actual sources support only the listed facts; the diagram and teaching construction each retain their own status."},"openQuestions":["This lesson established or ran no actual G/H-instance and reports no new mechanism support."],"taskId":"S-13-2","sources":[{"id":"T-main","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§13.2, Chinese source lines 2696–2701","claim_support":["Parallel use, minimum fields, G/H routes, and interface-card boundaries."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"T-U","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§3.5, Chinese source lines 495–542","claim_support":["General U01–U11 definitions and applicability ceilings."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"T-HV","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§7.3.1–7.3.11, Chinese source lines 1532–1595","claim_support":["Human-interface route levels: candidate/basic description differs from empirical elevation."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"T-fields","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§7.3, Chinese source lines 1524–1531","claim_support":["Formal dependencies, conditional routes, and minimum necessary information."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"T-Hinst","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§4.2, Chinese source lines 586–601","claim_support":["H1/H4/H5-instance eligibility and zero-conclusion gates."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"T-Hroles","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§7.1, Chinese source lines 1469–1485","claim_support":["The six human propositions have different roles."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"T-Ginst","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§4.1, Chinese source lines 557–585","claim_support":["G-instance pre-outcome contracts and evidence stages."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"P-NASA","title":"Report of Apollo 13 Review Board","url":"https://ntrs.nasa.gov/api/citations/19700076776/downloads/19700076776.pdf","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"June 1970","locator":"June 1970, printed pp.1-1–1-3,2-1–2-2; PDF pp.14–16,32–33.","claim_support":["The report records the Board's task and arrangements for MSC teams to supply factual data, analysis, and coordination."],"limits":["Documents may support role and interface inventories, not G2 channel causal effects, H5 persistent traces, or responsibility conclusions; they also provide no pre-outcome instance registration under this framework."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"theory-v2","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v2, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v2 / 2026-11-16","locator":"13.2 How to use general primitives and human-variable interfaces (11+11); Chinese source lines 2750–2755; SHA-256 6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v2; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-13-2/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-13-2/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-13-2/source/S-13-2.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-13-2/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text and section locations correspond to v2; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"a01ba288b23adb8f222273f84aa7b4bdf9b0b41bde4defbb5afdb88bf9d63fee","source_sha256":"6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c"}},"13.3":{"title":"13.3 Seven gates: the sole main process","lead":"The seven gates are not seven checkboxes, but recording responsibilities for one proposition checked along its dependencies. Later approval cannot turn missing earlier material into something present.","explanation":["Source location: Chinese source lines 2756–2775, original-site location /theory/part-13#section-13-3. See the theory-v2 source for the current full text; historical research originals and extracts remain downloadable in their original editions.","The source calls the seven gates the “sole main process” in translation. The run unit is a particular proposition and dependencies, not one label for the whole project. If a gate fails, dependent later gates are not_run and retain downgraded ceilings; genuinely independent judgments require their own evidence and use review. Failure of global premises—source status, common facts, object boundaries, or lawful use—stops every dependent output. Substantive changes require rerunning from the earliest affected gate, not merely editing the final conclusion. [T-main]","IG1, the effective-object gate, checks whether object, boundaries, nine-axis scale, window, and K suffice for this judgment. A name is insufficient, and an old object's contract may not cover a new use. Failure limits output to object redefinition, unknown scale, or conversion review; dependent causal and strong judgments cannot continue passing after object failure. [T-main]","IG2, evidence traceability, distinguishes sources, material, observations, evidence, counterexamples, cases, and judgments, making every elevation traceable. Facts must remain separate from their interpretation; a source's existence does not support arbitrary sentences. Failure preserves material organization, candidate questions, or hypotheses awaiting tests, not a continuing claim of sufficient evidence. [T-main]","IG3, affected positions, follows §14.4.1, distinguishing positions requiring complete listing, more distant positions warranting proportionate tracing, and unknowns that could change protection or comparison. Missing material is not no impact. Gaps relevant to a specific use limit the corresponding strong judgment and high-impact adoption. [theory-v2]","IG4, power and anti-retaliation, requires low-power positions to be able safely to add evidence, contest, refuse, stop, and appeal. An inbox is not safe expression, and intake is not ability to change outcomes; silence under retaliation concerns cannot support adverse inference. Failed protection reduces publication and judgment levels rather than deleting unheard people and declaring complete data. [T-main]","IG5, mediation and reflexivity, examines whether indicators, platforms, AI, public evaluations, or diagnosis itself alter evidence and objects. Publishing a score may change behavior and invalidate prior state assumptions. If high reflexivity cannot be bounded, use conditional state transitions with corresponding uncertainty rather than indiscriminately merging before/after observation data. [T-main]","IG6, strong-judgment procedure, triggers SJ1–SJ8 according to actual use and foreseeable effects. Public dissemination increases scrutiny but does not alone turn every argument into a strong judgment. Ordinary L1/L2 may be not_applicable with genuine specific reasons, without blocking IG7; passed requires the same run to bind the complete eight-item package, every item passed. An “already checked” string is no substitute. High-impact uses that trigger it with missing items must be failed, not falsely inapplicable. [T-main; T-SJ]","IG7, action ceiling, records only the maximum demands that may be proposed, prohibitions, stopping conditions, appeals, and rollback. It may conclude description, an evidence gap, inaction, or continued deliberation, not tool-generated authorization. External norms and permissions need independent traceable references; seven gates cannot spontaneously create them. [T-main; T-record]","Completing all applicable gates shows no gaps in those recording gates, not that the proposition is true. Empirical conclusions remain bound by instance and proposition contracts, normative targets need N premises, and real action still proceeds through choice, C12, J, and O. A procedurally complete conclusion later overturned by evidence illustrates why withdrawal conditions matter, not why procedures may be abandoned. [T-main]","Knight Capital historical material contrasts “checks exist” with “effects are evaluated.” The SEC order's control-assessment and review passages record gaps between control inventories and actual failure-risk evaluation. SEC procedures are not renamed IG here, nor is adding seven gates claimed necessarily to prevent an accident. It illustrates only that process names and existence records do not replace substantive checking. [P-SEC]","v2 explicitly connects IG3 to §14.4.1. For explanatory tasks not involving choice, tracing may stop when continuing could no longer reasonably alter the conclusion, judgment level, use, or protections. Adoption tasks must also ask whether unknowns could reveal protection failure or change option comparison. This reduction standard does not apply to direct or known low-power positions; affected strong judgments and uses remain limited by actual gaps."],"argumentSteps":["Fix proposition, object, use, and dependency graph, recording actual grounds gate by gate.","IG1–IG2 check objects and evidence first; IG3–IG5 check affected positions, protection, and reflexivity.","Determine IG6 applicability by use; passed must bind eight individually passed items.","IG7 sets only demands and ceilings, not authorization.","Failures propagate through dependencies; global gaps stop all affected outputs, and changes reopen the earliest affected point."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Main process","leftText":"Seven gates manage judgment handoffs uniformly.","rightTitle":"Expansion layer","rightText":"Adds detail without issuing competing passage."},{"leftTitle":"IG6 not applicable","leftText":"A low-impact use genuinely does not trigger it, with specific reasons.","rightTitle":"IG6 failed","rightText":"Triggered but material insufficient; it cannot be rewritten as inapplicable."},{"leftTitle":"Gate passed","leftText":"This recording responsibility meets its requirements.","rightTitle":"Fact true","rightText":"Determined by domain evidence and still withdrawable."}],"boundaries":["Source boundary: reasoned IG6 inapplicability does not block IG7; passed must bind complete SJ1–SJ8.","Source boundary: failure of a shared global premise stops all dependent outputs together.","Teaching counterexample: calling a personnel-eligibility score “ordinary opinion” leaves its actual use unchanged and still triggers the corresponding strong-judgment procedure."],"questions":["Does an ordinary public explanation automatically require the entire eight-item package?","If IG2 fails but IG7 has stopping clauses, may the whole chain be marked passed?","Who grants authorization after the seven gates?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-13-3/figures/gates-a.png","caseLinks":[{"title":"Knight Capital: control inventories versus control assessment","href":"https://www.sec.gov/files/litigation/admin/2013/34-70694.pdf"}],"caseNotes":[{"title":"Knight Capital: control inventories versus control assessment","text":"Paragraphs 28–32 of the 2013 SEC order identify scope gaps in Knight's control assessment and subsequent review, with personnel differing on whether procedures required evaluating controls or merely identifying their existence.","sourceLabel":"SEC administrative order: In the Matter of Knight Capital Americas LLC, Release 70694","sourceUrl":"https://www.sec.gov/files/litigation/admin/2013/34-70694.pdf","sourceLocator":"2013-10-16; page 1 for procedural status; pp.8–10, ¶28–34, on control assessment, documentation, review, and certification.","boundary":"Uses only historical review gaps stated in the regulatory order; does not equate those procedures with Xikari's seven gates or judge current law or accident-prevention effects.","href":"https://www.sec.gov/files/litigation/admin/2013/34-70694.pdf"}],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"13.3","start_line":2836,"end_line":2855,"startLine":2836,"endLine":2855}],"outputId":"section-13-3","sourceHash":"51795bb7988f2669a324a52666c320cfba1382f597f7b5c6a1431844572da1fb","teachingExamples":[{"title":"Teaching construction: two different status chains","text":"Proposition A would accuse an artificial organization of dereliction. IG1 verifies the object, but IG2 source-status verification fails. Dependent IG3–IG7 are all not_run; even a complete separate eight-item package cannot repair the source break. Proposition B only compares two public definitions in a low-impact classroom setting, with no intervention, eligibility, resource, or substantive reputational consequences for a specific subject. With evidence for other applicable gates, IG6 may be not_applicable for that explicit use reason, continuing to IG7 with description_only. B still has no action authorization.","assumptions":"Propositions, organization, uses, and statuses are artificial, not actual process certification.","boundary":"Retaining B depends on genuine independence; it cannot copy A's accusation under a new name."},{"title":"Teaching construction: where to reopen after a use change","text":"An artificial L2 report originally compares scheduling models, then is proposed for named employees' promotions. Recheck object and material fit for the new use and reopen at least affected positions, anti-retaliation, reflexivity, and strong-judgment procedures; if object definition changes, restart IG1. Renaming the report L3 or adding a signature on the final page is insufficient.","assumptions":"No actual employees, promotions, or automated decisions.","boundary":"Illustrates reopening at the earliest affected gate, without issuing intervention authority to any organization."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Source boundary: reasoned IG6 inapplicability does not block IG7; passed must bind complete SJ1–SJ8.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Source boundary: failure of a shared global premise stops all dependent outputs together.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Teaching counterexample: calling a personnel-eligibility score “ordinary opinion” leaves its actual use unchanged and still triggers the corresponding strong-judgment procedure.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"Does an ordinary public explanation automatically require the entire eight-item package?","answer":"Not automatically. Examine object, identifiability, accusations, dissemination, and actual consequences; publicity raises scrutiny.","basis":"IG6 applicability rule."},{"question":"If IG2 fails but IG7 has stopping clauses, may the whole chain be marked passed?","answer":"No. Later clauses do not repair earlier evidence breaks; dependent later gates are not_run.","basis":"Dependency semantics."},{"question":"Who grants authorization after the seven gates?","answer":"The tool does not; valid choice, C12, J, and O procedures need separate checks.","basis":"Passing all gates still grants no authorization."}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-13-3/figures/gates-a.png","alt":"Objects, evidence, affected positions, and anti-retaliation are four distinct gate checks.","caption":"Based on §13.3; a field's existence is not substantive passage."},{"path":"/reading-assets/S-13-3/figures/gates-b.png","alt":"Mediation, strong judgment, and action ceilings, alongside different requirements for IG6 inapplicability and passage.","caption":"Publicity does not automatically trigger every strong-judgment requirement; the tool creates no authorization."},{"path":"/reading-assets/S-13-3/figures/states.png","alt":"Artificial A has source failure followed by not_run; artificial B has reasoned low-impact IG6 inapplicability and can proceed to IG7.","caption":"Status examples, not real gate-check results."}],"diagramBrief":"Two static figures list IG1–IG4 and IG5–IG7 with the IG6 exception; a third shows artificial failure/inapplicability chains. Future interaction may display dependency propagation without turning unrun into passed.","applicability":{"realCase":"applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"This unit concerns methodological judgment. Actual sources support only the listed facts; the diagram and teaching construction each retain their own status."},"openQuestions":["No actual high-impact evaluation was run here; this historical case does not test the net effects of the seven gates."],"taskId":"S-13-3","sources":[{"id":"T-main","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§13.3, Chinese source lines 2702–2719","claim_support":["Seven individual gates, dependency failure, applicability exception, rerunning, and boundaries of all-gate passage."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"T-SJ","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§13.5, Chinese source lines 2752–2768","claim_support":["Uses and consequences trigger the eight-item strong-judgment package."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"T-record","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§13.9, Chinese source lines 2802–2813","claim_support":["Closed status and action-ceiling vocabulary; no self-generated authorization."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"P-SEC","title":"SEC administrative order: In the Matter of Knight Capital Americas LLC, Release 70694","url":"https://www.sec.gov/files/litigation/admin/2013/34-70694.pdf","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"2013-10-16","locator":"2013-10-16; page 1 for procedural status; pp.8–10, ¶28–34, on control assessment, documentation, review, and certification.","claim_support":["The SEC order states that initial assessment focused on existing control inventories and intended functions without adequately considering SMARS malfunction; later procedures were understood differently as evaluating controls or merely confirming existence."],"limits":["Regulatory findings in a settled administrative order, with the respondent neither admitting nor denying except jurisdictional matters; not an independent incident reconstruction here or proof that Xikari's seven gates could prevent this event."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"theory-v2","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v2, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v2 / 2026-11-16","locator":"13.3 Seven gates: the sole main process; Chinese source lines 2756–2775; SHA-256 6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v2; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-13-3/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-13-3/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-13-3/source/S-13-3.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-13-3/diagrams.md","editionNote":"The relevant explanation in this section has been revised for v2; the original research attachments and the scope of external-source review retain their historical status.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"799fcdef855a14bbadc3c5f80dd56f22ae590a23e2abb4962039871e18c3ed00","source_sha256":"6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c"}},"13.4":{"title":"13.4 Five gates and thirteen steps: the diagnostic expansion layer","lead":"Five gates and thirteen steps lay out material for complex questions, helping the seven gates see grounds clearly. They cannot issue another pass that bypasses the main process.","explanation":["Source location: Chinese source lines 2776–2807, original-site location /theory/part-13#section-13-4. See the theory-v2 source for the current full text; historical research originals and extracts remain downloadable in their original editions.","The source positions this as a “diagnostic expansion layer” in translation. Use it for complex objects, high responsibility, high reflexivity, or when seven-gate review needs finer evidence gathering—not for thirteen mandatory steps in every proofreading task. Outputs of the five local gates and thirteen steps return to the sole seven-gate process, creating no second main-process status. [T-main; T-seven]","Preserve gate mappings individually: FG1 object material serves IG1; FG2 signal costs, sources, weights, and review serve IG2; FG3 nine axes, within-scale objects/evidence, and scale change serve both IG1/IG2; FG4 effects on rights/resources, contrary conditions, repair windows, and appeal/review serve IG3/IG4/IG6; FG5 pre-observation baseline, responses during observation and after publication, and recursion caps serve IG5. Calling FG4 a responsibility gate does not allocate responsibility by itself. FG5 stops endless recursive checking rather than allowing “observe the observer again” to expand indefinitely. [T-main]","DS01, question entry, first states in ordinary language who asks what and for which use. “How are erroneous records corrected?” should not begin as “this system belongs to a certain phase.” DS02, object and boundaries, then identifies effective objects, subsystems, higher-level environments, and external channels to bound comparison; objects without corresponding material remain candidates. [T-main]","DS03 separates facts, behavior, resources, feedback, narratives, feelings, conjectures, and noise. One interview may include personal experience and speculation about others; not all of it can be labeled fact or noise. DS04, observation participation, registers observed people's awareness, observer power, publication effects, and possible responses. This concerns both evidence generation and safe expression; people aware of being scored cannot be treated as static objects unaffected by observation. [T-main]","DS05, dominant constraints, retains candidates, selection reasons, and competing constraints. “Dominant” is a contestable task judgment, not selecting the most theory-like cause first. DS06, states and windows, uses S0–S6 only for applicable human intentional collectives, also recording within-state positions, parallel subsystems, and contrary conditions. Technical systems and categories lacking intentionality grounds must not be forced into them; state inapplicability and use the domain's own states and windows. Stages are not maturity or destiny. [T-main; T-HV10]","DS07, core structural scan, checks anchors, uptake layers, transmission chains, boundaries, and feedback write-back. Naming a link does not establish a connected chain; every link needs object and channel grounds. DS08, key protection variables, lists protected objects, damage paths, resources, redundancy, and repair/return paths. Finding importance generates protection needs, not immediate authority for researchers to alter resources or decide for people. [T-main]","DS09, uptake and repayment, separates cost bearers, return recipients, stopping rights, long-term overdraft, and harm transfer. The busiest, most visible, or final-step executor does not thereby become solely responsible or obliged to keep sacrificing. DS10, hidden problems and boundary types, checks maintenance debt, fast/slow mismatch, indicator substitution, distorted summaries, and object-conversion candidates. Each is a checkable question; a full terminology list does not establish every problem occurred. [T-main]","DS11 lists only competing mechanism candidates allowed by material and sufficient to change judgment, stating needed and weakening evidence and elimination rules. Without reasonable alternatives, report search scope and failure conditions rather than inventing stories to fill two or three mechanisms. Candidates remain limited by causal contracts; procedural generation does not make explanations empirical evidence. [T-main; T-causal]","DS12 branch anticipation lists triggers, early signals, reversal conditions, repair windows, and minimum action requirements. Branches are not determined futures, and minimum requirements are not direct execution commands. DS13 outputs in ordinary language with revision triggers, record scope, review, withdrawal, and rollback; without effective review and withdrawal, judgments cannot be entered into organizational records, qualifications, allocations, or public memory. A new explanation also does not automatically correct the downstream effects of old records. [T-main]","The added point from the Knight Capital case here is that accident analysis must separate deployment, request transmission, monitoring, and response. The SEC order lists them separately and records a code-withdrawal operation that worsened the failure. This helps explain why technical states, response timing, and repair conditions need separate columns; it assigns no technical system an S0—S6 label or all responsibility to one person based on their execution position. [P-SEC]","The strongest objection is that thirteen steps become lengthy paperwork. Reuse the same evidence through stable references, explain decisive reasons in the main text, and have procedural checks handle repeated fields; still retain unknowns and conflicts that actually affect the conclusion. Completing every local step neither automatically passes the seven gates nor proves an actual option optimal. Use and materials determine stopping points. [T-main]"],"argumentSteps":["Decide whether expansion is needed, preserving the seven gates as the sole source of status.","FG1—FG5 map details back to corresponding main gates without issuing another overall pass.","DS01—DS04 specify the question, objects, signals, and observation effects.","DS05—DS10 check constraints, applicable states, structure, protection, costs, and hidden hazards.","DS11—DS13 form bounded competing mechanisms and conditional branches, outputting reviewable, retractable text with a defined scope."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Local expansion gate","leftText":"Checks material details and feeds them back to the main gate.","rightTitle":"Second main process","rightText":"This section prohibits creating competing statuses."},{"leftTitle":"Protection need","leftText":"Identifies risks and required conditions.","rightTitle":"Authority to take protective action","rightText":"Requires valid external grounds; need does not generate authority."},{"leftTitle":"Candidate mechanism","leftText":"An explanation that evidence can eliminate.","rightTitle":"Causal conclusion","rightText":"Separately satisfies the causal contract and corresponding instance."},{"leftTitle":"Write-back","leftText":"Actually changes the corresponding record/state.","rightTitle":"New document","rightText":"May merely add text without repairing old effects."}],"boundaries":["Boundary in the source: DS06 must not force S0—S6 onto inapplicable objects; applicable collectives are also not ordered by maturity or destiny.","Boundary in the source: DS09 does not turn uptake and overextension into obligations to sacrifice or total responsibility.","Boundary in the source: DS11 does not invent mechanisms to meet a numerical requirement.","Boundary in the source: without review and withdrawal, DS13 must not enter organizational records, qualifications, allocation, or public memory."],"questions":["Must a technical-system failure be written as S0—S6?","Are three listed mechanisms more qualified than one?","If key protection variables have been identified, may others' resources be allocated directly?","If all thirteen steps are filled, may a failed seven-gate check be ignored?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-13-4/figures/fg.png","caseLinks":[{"title":"Why Knight accident materials are recorded in layers","href":"https://www.sec.gov/files/litigation/admin/2013/34-70694.pdf"}],"caseNotes":[{"title":"Why Knight accident materials are recorded in layers","text":"The SEC's 2013 order separately records omitted code deployment, failure to carry order-system information onward, limits of monitoring tools, and on-site response; ¶27 records that removing new code from seven servers worsened the problem.","sourceLabel":"SEC：Knight Capital Americas LLC，Release 70694","sourceUrl":"https://www.sec.gov/files/litigation/admin/2013/34-70694.pdf","sourceLocator":"2013-10-16, page 1; pages 6—7, ¶15—19; page 8, ¶25—27.","boundary":"This explains only the significance of recording chains and repair timing; it does not claim the parties followed the thirteen steps or determine all responsibility from one technical operation.","href":"https://www.sec.gov/files/litigation/admin/2013/34-70694.pdf"}],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"13.4","start_line":2856,"end_line":2887,"startLine":2856,"endLine":2887}],"outputId":"section-13-4","sourceHash":"51795bb7988f2669a324a52666c320cfba1382f597f7b5c6a1431844572da1fb","teachingExamples":[{"title":"Teaching construction: a minimum thirteen-step chain for an incorrect roster","text":"The artificial request is ‘explain why this week's roster assigns A twice and propose review conditions’, limited to internal error checking. DS01 records the request and use; DS02 bounds roster v1, the importer, and the approval interface; DS03 separates observation of two duplicate rows from speculation about ‘deliberate overassignment’; DS04 records that publishing a named conclusion may affect personnel; DS05 compares duplicate import and conflicting merge as two candidate constraints; DS06 does not apply S0—S6 to a technical table, recording v1/v2 and time instead; DS07 traces the chain from file to operational roster; DS08 protects actual rest and correction opportunities; DS09 lists who checks, who bears temporary coordination costs, and rights to stop; DS10 checks old-version caches; DS11 uses import IDs and version logs to distinguish candidates rather than judging motives through guesses; DS12 proposes evidence supplementation and correction requirements if duplicate IDs are confirmed, otherwise retaining an unresolved status; DS13 outputs only a reviewable report and reopening conditions, without changing an actual roster.","assumptions":"People, tables, and logs are artificial; the candidates suffice to change the handling direction, but no actual system was run.","boundary":"This construction displays each step's products, not a seven-gate pass, established causation, or authorization to change schedules."},{"title":"Teaching construction: the stopping point when independent review is missing","text":"An artificial organization wants to enter the preceding explanation into A's qualification file, but only the diagnosis's author can change the conclusion, A cannot supplement evidence, and old versions cannot be withdrawn. DS13 conditions are insufficient, so this high-impact entry should stop. Even complete DS01—DS12 materials cannot use expansion-layer ‘completion’ to bypass the main process.","assumptions":"No actual personnel or qualification record is involved.","boundary":"This does not claim all internal material organization must stop; only the high-impact use depending on the missing item stops."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Boundary in the source: DS06 must not force S0—S6 onto inapplicable objects; applicable collectives are also not ordered by maturity or destiny.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Boundary in the source: DS09 does not turn uptake and overextension into obligations to sacrifice or total responsibility.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Boundary in the source: DS11 does not invent mechanisms to meet a numerical requirement.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Boundary in the source: without review and withdrawal, DS13 must not enter organizational records, qualifications, allocation, or public memory.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"Must a technical-system failure be written as S0—S6?","answer":"Do not force it; state inapplicability and retain the domain's own versions, states, and windows.","basis":"DS06。"},{"question":"Are three listed mechanisms more qualified than one?","answer":"Not necessarily. Candidates must be reasonable and potentially change the judgment; do not invent them to reach a count.","basis":"DS11。"},{"question":"If key protection variables have been identified, may others' resources be allocated directly?","answer":"No. Only protection needs are generated; execution authority requires separate checking.","basis":"DS08。"},{"question":"If all thirteen steps are filled, may a failed seven-gate check be ignored?","answer":"No. The expansion layer has no second set of pass statuses.","basis":"This section's opening paragraph."}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-13-4/figures/fg.png","alt":"The five FG gates map respectively to IG1, IG2, IG1/2, IG3/4/6, and IG5.","caption":"The source's mapping, with no new main-process status."},{"path":"/reading-assets/S-13-4/figures/ds-a.png","alt":"DS01 through DS07: questions, objects, signals, observation, constraints, states, and structure.","caption":"A static product checklist, not facts already obtained."},{"path":"/reading-assets/S-13-4/figures/ds-b.png","alt":"DS08 through DS13: protection, costs, hidden hazards, mechanisms, branches, and retractable output.","caption":"Needs and candidates generate neither obligations, causation, nor execution authority."}],"diagramBrief":"Three static diagrams list the five-gate mapping, DS01—DS07, and DS08—DS13. Future interaction would only expand each step's minimum product and failure ceiling, without automatically issuing a main-process pass.","applicability":{"realCase":"applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"This unit concerns methodological judgment. Actual sources support only the listed facts; the diagram and teaching construction each retain their own status."},"openQuestions":["No organizational diagnosis, personnel survey, or roster correction was performed, and the process's net benefits were not evaluated."],"taskId":"S-13-4","sources":[{"id":"T-main","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§13.4, Chinese source lines 2720–2751","claim_support":["FG1—FG5 mappings, DS01—DS13 products, and each boundary."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"T-seven","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§13.3, Chinese source lines 2702–2719","claim_support":["The seven gates as the sole main process."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"T-HV10","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"Chinese source, §7.3.10, lines 1586—1589","claim_support":["S0—S6 applicability, not maturity."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"T-causal","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§2.4, Chinese source lines 293–298","claim_support":["Candidate mechanisms and causal-evidence contracts."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"P-SEC","title":"SEC：Knight Capital Americas LLC，Release 70694","url":"https://www.sec.gov/files/litigation/admin/2013/34-70694.pdf","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"2013-10-16","locator":"2013-10-16, page 1; pages 6—7, ¶15—19; page 8, ¶25—27.","claim_support":["The SEC order separately describes deployment omissions, order transmission, monitoring limitations, and accident response; removing new code from seven servers worsened the problem."],"limits":["Regulatory findings in a settled administrative order, not this lesson's independent accident reconstruction; no technical system is fitted into human S0—S6, and execution position does not independently assign responsibility."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"theory-v2","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v2, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v2 / 2026-11-16","locator":"13.4 Five gates and thirteen steps: the diagnostic expansion layer; Chinese source, lines 2776—2807; SHA-256 6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v2; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-13-4/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-13-4/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-13-4/source/S-13-4.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-13-4/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text and section locations correspond to v2; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"172e061b4fc0dda73cebf4761acaf163060513faee5f372469ddcebe1d47cae3","source_sha256":"6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c"}},"13.5":{"title":"13.5 The eight-part strong-judgment package","lead":"The ‘strong’ in strong judgment concerns its use, not how confidently it is worded. A score affecting qualifications, rights, or reputation must permit supplemental evidence, refutation, and actual correction even if called a draft.","explanation":["Chinese source location: lines 2808—2824; original-site location /theory/part-13#section-13-5. The current complete text is in the theory-v2 source; the historical research manuscript and excerpts remain available in their original versions for download and checking.","The source defines strong judgment by ‘use and foreseeable effects’ (translated from Chinese). Judgments intended or reasonably foreseeable for action upon people, qualifications, rights, resource allocation, material effects on specific parties' reputations and public memory, or incorporation into automated, organized, or institutional decisions enter the corresponding procedure. Intermediate scores, recommendations, or material filtering also require actual-use checks if transmitted into such decisions; an upstream ‘for reference only’ label does not automatically remove downstream responsibilities. [T-main]","Public dissemination heightens scrutiny but is not sufficient to upgrade every public analysis to L3. Consider the object, factual allegations, identifiability, scope, and consequences. Ordinary argument evaluation, mechanism explanation, or normative opinion can still give clear grounded conclusions when it does not reach those effects. Conversely, titles such as ‘commentary’, ‘research’, or ‘draft’ do not exempt uses actually meeting strong-judgment conditions. [T-main]","SJ1 reversal conditions ask which observable circumstances would weaken, reverse, or withdraw a judgment, not merely ‘we will reconsider if something changes’. SJ2 repair windows ask when correction remains possible, on what grounds, and when it expires. A window cannot be set by wish; if lost qualifications or opportunities are hard to recover, record that irreversibility in the ceiling rather than assuming a later apology suffices. [T-main]","SJ3 evidence requirements separate support, missingness, and contrary evidence. Contrary materials are not decorative appendices; they may change the judgment. SJ4 appeal entry specifies who can enter, who is responsible, processing time, and whether results can change. A mailing address without any possibility of changing records or pausing consequences is not an effective appeal. [T-main]","SJ5 protection against retaliation must define protected conduct, monitoring, independent reporting, and remedies. Merely writing ‘retaliation prohibited’ does not establish that low-power positions can actually speak safely. SJ6 the right to supplement evidence must permit submission, version correction, and downstream synchronization, rather than changing one database while other scores and rosters still implement the old error. Arrange privacy protection and checkable identities by matter; disclosure of all materials cannot be a condition of appeal. [T-main]","SJ7 external-review triggers specify when review starts, who is independent, and whether sufficient materials can be accessed and consequences stopped. A nominal outsider dependent on the assessed party's control of materials, personnel, or dismissal may lack substantive independence; check actual arrangements rather than organizational names. SJ8 rollback and write-back specify the state to return to, who repairs it, the authoritative version, and synchronization. Rollback may not restore already lost opportunities, so residual harms remain separately recorded. [T-main]","Only L3 with all seven gates and all eight items passed permits registering a strong-judgment candidate, still without actual authorization to act. Any missing item fails that judgment's IG6, preventing retention of the original high-impact use. It may be reduced to an internal hypothesis, open assertion, or candidate mechanism within the materials and actual uses allowed, not renamed while continuing the same action upon people. [T-main；T-seven]","Independent L2 comparisons that do not depend on the missing item or trigger strong judgment may remain, preventing local failure from canceling all materials; if a common premise fails, the dependent chain stops together. The eight items are not mutually compensable scores: seven perfect scores and one missing appeal do not qualify through a high average. Strong factual support also cannot offset protection or review gaps. [T-main]","The added point from the Knight order here is that ‘independence’ requires institutional conditions. The historical document requires consultant review and also specifies access to materials, implementation certification, follow-up checks, and restrictions on arbitrary dismissal and later relationships. It illustrates why review cannot stop at a title; the order's text proves neither implementation nor satisfaction of all SJ1—SJ8. [P-SEC]","The strongest objection is that eight items make emergencies too slow to address. Check waiting costs, the narrowest reversible requirements, monitoring, and valid external authority separately, without deleting objects, protection, or stopping conditions. Incomplete items lower the corresponding strong-judgment use; emergencies neither let tools create power nor force independently established low-impact descriptions into silence. [T-main；T-level]"],"argumentSteps":["Determine triggering by use and foreseeable consequences, not tone or title.","Submit SJ1—SJ8 item by item, supporting contrary evidence, appeals, supplemental evidence, external review, and actual repair.","Judge each independently without compensating for missing items through an overall score.","A missing item fails the relevant IG6, stopping strong judgment and its original high-impact use; independent L2 is reviewed separately.","Even completeness yields only a strong-judgment candidate; actual action still requires choice, C12, J, and O checks."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Strong tone","leftText":"A manner of expression.","rightTitle":"Strong judgment","rightText":"Use or foreseeable consequences reach the specified responsibilities."},{"leftTitle":"Formal appeal","leftText":"An email can be submitted.","rightTitle":"Effective appeal","rightText":"Responsible parties, time limits, and actual capacity to change records/actions exist."},{"leftTitle":"New version published","leftText":"New text appears.","rightTitle":"Actual rollback and repair","rightText":"Authoritative records, dependent objects, and residual consequences are addressed."}],"boundaries":["Boundary in the source: publicity is insufficient to upgrade all analysis uniformly to L3.","Boundary in the source: any missing package item blocks the corresponding strong judgment; renaming or splitting cannot preserve its original use.","Boundary in the source: an external title is not independence, and apology is not actual rollback."],"questions":["A score is called a ‘draft’ but will automatically determine qualifications. Is the procedure triggered?","If seven items pass and one is missing, may a majority pass be used?","Is a publicly published pure mathematical derivation automatically L3?","Does an external expert's signature suffice to establish independent review?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-13-5/figures/sj-a.png","caseLinks":[{"title":"Independent review must specify usable conditions","href":"https://www.sec.gov/files/litigation/admin/2013/34-70694.pdf"}],"caseNotes":[{"title":"Independent review must specify usable conditions","text":"Paragraph ¶51 of the 2013 Knight order specifies an independent consultant's material access, implementation certification, and follow-up review, with restrictions on dismissal and later business relationships.","sourceLabel":"SEC：Knight Capital Americas LLC，Release 70694","sourceUrl":"https://www.sec.gov/files/litigation/admin/2013/34-70694.pdf","sourceLocator":"2013-10-16, page 1; pages 14—17, ¶51A—M, especially B, H, I, J, and K.","boundary":"Requirements in an order are not actual fulfillment; this illustrates institutional details of external review, without certifying all eight items or actual remedies.","href":"https://www.sec.gov/files/litigation/admin/2013/34-70694.pdf"}],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"13.5","start_line":2888,"end_line":2904,"startLine":2888,"endLine":2904}],"outputId":"section-13-5","sourceHash":"51795bb7988f2669a324a52666c320cfba1382f597f7b5c6a1431844572da1fb","teachingExamples":[{"title":"Teaching construction: 7/8 does not pass","text":"An artificial qualification score is intended for personnel files. Seven items have sufficient arrangements, but SJ4 has only an email address, no responsible party, and nobody able to change the score. SJ4 and the relevant IG6 should fail, and the score cannot retain its qualification use; even 7/8=87.5% is not the admission criterion. A separate anonymous process comparison independent of that score may be reviewed on its own evidence and low-impact use.","assumptions":"Scores, files, and people are artificial; sufficiency of the other seven items is stipulated and certifies no actual system.","boundary":"This displays a conjunctive gate, not actual personnel selection or qualification adjudication."},{"title":"Teaching construction: corrected text without a restored opportunity","text":"An artificial report wrongly lists B as absent. After a correction is published, the internal roster changes, but B's missed candidate interview is not restored. One may accurately say the text and roster were corrected, not that every consequence was repaired. The original task must specify whether the interview opportunity is restored, who handles it, and remaining limits; if authority is still absent, propose only the missing conditions.","assumptions":"No actual B or interview event exists; correction and opportunity are separate endpoints.","boundary":"Not every remedy requires return to the original historical state; objectives, authority, and practical feasibility still require checking."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Boundary in the source: publicity is insufficient to upgrade all analysis uniformly to L3.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Boundary in the source: any missing package item blocks the corresponding strong judgment; renaming or splitting cannot preserve its original use.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Boundary in the source: an external title is not independence, and apology is not actual rollback.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"A score is called a ‘draft’ but will automatically determine qualifications. Is the procedure triggered?","answer":"Yes. The actual use meets strong-judgment conditions.","basis":"The opening paragraph's use criterion."},{"question":"If seven items pass and one is missing, may a majority pass be used?","answer":"No. All eight items are required individually; the relevant IG6 fails.","basis":"Conjunctive admission."},{"question":"Is a publicly published pure mathematical derivation automatically L3?","answer":"Not automatically; judge specific parties, uses, and foreseeable effects together.","basis":"The qualification concerning publicity."},{"question":"Does an external expert's signature suffice to establish independent review?","answer":"Insufficient. Materials, timing, independence, and effective stopping authority also matter.","basis":"SJ7 and its boundary."}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-13-5/figures/sj-a.png","alt":"Four items: reversal conditions, repair windows, evidence requirements, and effective appeals.","caption":"Based on §13.5; a name's existence is not an actual arrangement."},{"path":"/reading-assets/S-13-5/figures/sj-b.png","alt":"Four items: protection against retaliation, supplemental evidence, independent external review, and rollback/write-back.","caption":"Records, authority, and residual consequences require separate checks."},{"path":"/reading-assets/S-13-5/figures/sj-rule.png","alt":"Actual use triggers the procedure; L3 plus seven gates and eight passed items yields only a strong-judgment candidate, without execution authority.","caption":"Artificial 7/8 counterexample: a percentage is not the admission criterion."}],"diagramBrief":"Two static diagrams list SJ1—4 and SJ5—8; a third emphasizes use-based triggering and missing-item failure. Future interaction may show missing items blocking qualification, without using progress percentages as strong-judgment permission.","applicability":{"realCase":"applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"This unit concerns methodological judgment. Actual sources support only the listed facts; the diagram and teaching construction each retain their own status."},"openQuestions":["All subsequent fulfillment materials for the order were not checked, and completed remediation is not claimed; this lesson made no actual strong judgment or qualification decision."],"taskId":"S-13-5","sources":[{"id":"T-main","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§13.5, Chinese source lines 2752–2768","claim_support":["Use-based triggering, item-by-item SJ requirements, and boundaries for failure/independent L2."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"T-seven","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§13.3, Chinese source lines 2702–2719","claim_support":["IG6 is tied to complete SJ and the seven-gate output ceiling."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"T-level","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"Chinese source, §13.6, lines 2769—2778","claim_support":["Emergencies do not delete object, protection, or stopping conditions."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"P-SEC","title":"SEC：Knight Capital Americas LLC，Release 70694","url":"https://www.sec.gov/files/litigation/admin/2013/34-70694.pdf","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"2013-10-16","locator":"2013-10-16, page 1; pages 14—17, ¶51A—M, especially B, H, I, J, and K.","claim_support":["The order lists requirements for independent-consultant access to records and personnel, implementation certification, follow-up review, dismissal restrictions, and later-relationship restrictions."],"limits":["These are requirements in that historical order, not proof of actual completion; the material does not cover all eight items of this framework and is not used for current legal advice or recommendations for action upon individuals."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"theory-v2","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v2, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v2 / 2026-11-16","locator":"13.5 The eight-part strong-judgment package; Chinese source, lines 2808—2824; SHA-256 6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v2; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-13-5/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-13-5/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-13-5/source/S-13-5.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-13-5/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text and section locations correspond to v2; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"a223c05a954e315040b3ef95afd22ec2fd7774b4ae8ade8766db1668b06dd8fa","source_sha256":"6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c"}},"13.6":{"title":"13.6 Diagnostic levels","lead":"L1, L2, and L3 are levels of responsibility, not credibility rankings. The greater a task's effects, the more it needs traceable, refutable conditions and the ability to stop.","explanation":["Chinese source location: lines 2825—2834; original-site location /theory/part-13#section-13-6. The current complete text is in the theory-v2 source; the historical research manuscript and excerpts remain available in their original versions for download and checking.","The source says ‘a higher level is not closer to truth’ (translated from Chinese). The same fact may have a reliable low-impact description, while a complex high-impact judgment may remain factually uncertain. Levels mainly organize use, reversibility, and procedural responsibilities; L3 cannot become a badge of authority, nor L1 permission to write facts carelessly. [T-main]","L1 suits lightweight description and exploratory questions with low complexity, low impact, reversibility, and limited information. Output stops at descriptions, questions, and the next observation—for example, registering a passage's source or identifying a material gap without packaging observation as a mechanism conclusion. It clarifies unknowns rather than using limited information as a back door to high-impact judgment. [T-main]","L2 is a standard reviewable judgment requiring structural comparison without entering action carrying high responsibility. It may offer bounded comparative judgments or candidate mechanisms, with clear conclusions, grounds, and counterexamples, without deliberate vagueness. If intended for named qualification, resource, or automated decisions, actual use has changed; the L2 label cannot be retained while continuing issuance. [T-main；T-SJ]","L3 concerns substantively high-impact judgments and judgments embedded in automated, organized, or institutional decisions; public dissemination also needs review of objects, factual accusations, identifiability, and consequences. Its ceiling is a strong-judgment candidate after the seven gates and eight-part package pass, not final action permission. Selecting L3 cannot skip gates or turn ‘stricter review will occur’ into ‘already passed’. [T-main; T-seven]","Downgrade triggers include reduced evidence coverage, object conversion, uncontrolled reflexivity, and failed appeal or anti-retaliation protections. Downgrading must change actual output and use, stopping dependent conclusions where needed; changing the cover from L3 to L2 while the automated pipeline keeps using it does not suffice. Object or use changes require review from the earliest affected gate, preserving old records and reasons. [T-main; T-seven]","Urgency removes neither object, protection, nor stopping conditions. Where waiting is costly, separately propose the narrowest withdrawable, monitorable action requirement, still subject to corresponding external authorization; ‘act first, fill in later’ is disallowed. If actual conditions prevent withdrawal, state irreversibility and limits rather than making an irreversible act reversible through wording. [T-main; T-record]","The Knight case adds that emergency response is not automatically correct. The SEC order records continuing orders during on-site troubleshooting and a code rollback worsening the problem. Time pressure still requires separating diagnosis, actions, and consequences. This guide does not retrospectively assign L levels or claim that tier rules necessarily prevent accidents. [P-SEC]","The strongest objection is excessive procedure in ordinary discussion. Distinguish actual use: proportionately recorded low-impact explanation can be explicit without all public content becoming L3. Conversely, gently titled scoring used in automated decisions still needs impact-based treatment. Reducing unnecessary burden and preventing evasion of responsibility can coexist. [T-main; T-SJ]"],"argumentSteps":["Select the level by complexity, impact, reversibility, and actual use.","L1 retains descriptive questions; L2 offers bounded comparisons/mechanism candidates; L3 offers only strong-judgment candidates after itemwise gates.","Levels are neither truth values nor confidence; assess evidence separately.","Substantively downgrade and review use after coverage, object, reflexivity, or protection failures.","Urgency permits only the narrowest monitorable requirements; authority and stopping conditions remain."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Higher level","leftText":"More recording and protective responsibilities.","rightTitle":"Higher truth","rightText":"Empirical support strength cannot follow from level."},{"leftTitle":"Downgrade","leftText":"Reduce actual judgment/use and preserve failures.","rightTitle":"Relabel","rightText":"A textual replacement with unchanged use does not meet requirements."},{"leftTitle":"Action requirements","leftText":"Conditional advice and its ceiling.","rightTitle":"Actual execution","rightText":"Requires separate valid authorization and procedures."}],"boundaries":["Boundary in the original text: urgency cannot delete object, protection, or stopping conditions.","Boundary in the original text: L3’s ceiling remains a strong-judgment candidate and creates no authorization.","Teaching counterexample: L1 checking an explicit document date may be highly reliable, while an L3 risk score may remain uncertain despite extensive procedure."],"questions":["Is L3 necessarily more accurate than L2?","After appeal failure, is changing only the cover’s level enough?","Must public ordinary concept comparisons be L3?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-13-6/figures/levels.png","caseLinks":[{"title":"Responses under time pressure still need consequence checks","href":"https://www.sec.gov/files/litigation/admin/2013/34-70694.pdf"}],"caseNotes":[{"title":"Responses under time pressure still need consequence checks","text":"Paragraph 27 of the SEC’s 2013 Knight order records orders continuing during on-site troubleshooting; an attempt to remove new code from seven servers worsened the problem.","sourceLabel":"SEC：Knight Capital Americas LLC，Release 70694","sourceUrl":"https://www.sec.gov/files/litigation/admin/2013/34-70694.pdf","sourceLocator":"2013-10-16, p. 1 and p. 8, ¶27.","boundary":"Only a historical comparison separating diagnosis, emergency response, and actual consequences; no hindsight inevitability or tier-effectiveness inference.","href":"https://www.sec.gov/files/litigation/admin/2013/34-70694.pdf"}],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"13.6","start_line":2905,"end_line":2914,"startLine":2905,"endLine":2914}],"outputId":"section-13-6","sourceHash":"51795bb7988f2669a324a52666c320cfba1382f597f7b5c6a1431844572da1fb","teachingExamples":[{"title":"Teaching construction: the same analysis changes level with use","text":"Artificial task A describes two inconsistent dates in meeting minutes, using L1 and recommending checking originals. Task B compares two sources of inconsistency and forms bounded candidates under L2. Task C proposes deciding someone’s promotion on that basis, invoking L3 responsibilities; B’s ‘mechanism candidate’ cannot directly enter an eligibility pipeline. If C lacks effective appeal, stop the related strong judgment and high-impact use while retaining the independently established date inconsistency.","assumptions":"Meetings, people, and promotion are artificial, with no actual eligibility decision.","boundary":"The tier example establishes neither causes nor motives and does not mean all seven gates were run."},{"title":"Teaching construction: the narrowest requirement in an emergency","text":"An artificial system has unexplained duplicate writes, and the analyst lacks execution authority. A conditional requirement may say, ‘An authorized responsible party should determine whether to suspend a specific write queue while retaining read-only checks and confirmation of in-flight states’, listing needed evidence, monitoring, and restoration grounds. The analyst cannot claim a shutdown occurred or call permanent whole-system deletion withdrawable.","assumptions":"Artificial system, with no actual shutdown or deletion.","boundary":"Illustrates only separation of requirements and authority; actual safe withdrawal still depends on specific authorization and consequence assessment."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Boundary in the original text: urgency cannot delete object, protection, or stopping conditions.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Boundary in the original text: L3’s ceiling remains a strong-judgment candidate and creates no authorization.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Teaching counterexample: L1 checking an explicit document date may be highly reliable, while an L3 risk score may remain uncertain despite extensive procedure.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"Is L3 necessarily more accurate than L2?","answer":"Not necessarily. L3 indicates greater impact and procedural responsibilities; truth still depends on evidence.","basis":"The original text's final paragraph."},{"question":"After appeal failure, is changing only the cover’s level enough?","answer":"No. Stop or narrow related uses, preserve failures, and review dependencies.","basis":"Substantive downgrade."},{"question":"Must public ordinary concept comparisons be L3?","answer":"Not necessarily; consider object, use, and substantive impact.","basis":"Conditional review of public dissemination."}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-13-6/figures/levels.png","alt":"L1 descriptive questions, L2 bounded comparisons/candidate mechanisms, L3 strong-judgment candidates after gates.","caption":"Responsibility levels, not a truth ladder."},{"path":"/reading-assets/S-13-6/figures/downgrade.png","alt":"Failed coverage, object identity, reflexivity, appeal, or anti-retaliation triggers substantive downgrade; urgency does not waive authorization.","caption":"Based on §13.6; changing names cannot retain old uses."}],"diagramBrief":"Two static diagrams list three ceilings and downgrade triggers. Future interaction may change use to indicate added responsibilities, without treating a level slider as a truth score or authorization switch.","applicability":{"realCase":"applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"This unit concerns methodological judgment. Actual sources support only the listed facts; the diagram and teaching construction each retain their own status."},"openQuestions":["No actual institution was tiered or shut down and no eligibility judgment made; no new experiment supports tier effectiveness."],"taskId":"S-13-6","sources":[{"id":"T-main","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"Chinese source, §13.6, lines 2769—2778","claim_support":["Applicability/ceilings of three levels, downgrade triggers, and emergency boundaries."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"T-SJ","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§13.5, Chinese source lines 2752–2768","claim_support":["Strong judgments depend on use, not tone or a blanket public/private distinction."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"T-seven","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§13.3, Chinese source lines 2702–2719","claim_support":["Rerunning after use changes and IG6 responsibilities."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"T-record","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§13.9, Chinese source lines 2802–2813","claim_support":["Separate requirements, execution, and authorization."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"P-SEC","title":"SEC：Knight Capital Americas LLC，Release 70694","url":"https://www.sec.gov/files/litigation/admin/2013/34-70694.pdf","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"2013-10-16","locator":"2013-10-16, p. 1 and p. 8, ¶27.","claim_support":["The order records Knight continuing to send orders during troubleshooting and a code-removal response worsening the problem."],"limits":["Historical findings in a regulatory settlement order; not all emergency responses fail, and no Xikari level is thereby proved to prevent harm."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"theory-v2","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v2, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v2 / 2026-11-16","locator":"13.6 Diagnostic Levels; Chinese source lines 2825–2834; SHA-256 6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v2; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-13-6/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-13-6/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-13-6/source/S-13-6.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-13-6/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text and section locations correspond to v2; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"34f8b5122da421cd7ebca98bb19a4a47b117af4b184d6e9986cf20f9e0561cbb","source_sha256":"6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c"}},"13.7":{"title":"13.7 Six Formal Tools","lead":"Each of six tools manages a kind of record. They can locate one another, but cannot serve as one another’s external evidence and circulate an unverified judgment into fact.","explanation":["Source location: Chinese source lines 2835–2849, original-site location /theory/part-13#section-13-7. The current full text is in the theory-v2 source; the historical research draft and excerpts remain downloadable in their original versions.","These tools are formats and working methods for recording responsibilities, not a list of installed or necessarily executable software. Formal IDs provide stable references; names do not prove implemented functionality. Tools may share objects, sources, and versions while preserving material status and support scope. [T-main]","TOOL-EVIDENCE-LEDGER separates sources, material, observations, evidence, counterexamples, coverage, and uncertainty. It answers ‘Where are the grounds, which sentence do they support, and to what limit?’ A URL cannot mark a claim supported. Repeated references to one source remain one dependency, not independent validations. [T-main]","TOOL-OPEN-ASSERTION records a temporary judgment target open to challenge, falsification, and withdrawal. Explicitness makes it more testable than vague impressions but does not permit high-responsibility action. TOOL-CLAIM-VALIDATION further checks claim type, evidence, counterevidence, falsification conditions, impact, and ceilings; the former’s ‘registered’ cannot substitute for the latter’s substantive validation. [T-main]","TOOL-FORECAST-REGISTRY freezes predictions, windows, triggers, early and contrary signals, and revision targets. Freezing does not make a forecast correct; it prevents changed goals from contaminating later evaluation. Failed predictions must write back to related claims, not delete failed versions and claim they were always scenarios. Without the foresight contract, retain conditional-scenario status; a registry cannot generate eligibility for a hit. [T-main; T-dynamic]","TOOL-STRESS-TEST uses boundary cases, adversarial explanations, and scale changes to find failures and narrow scope. Internal constructions may refute unconditional logical leaps or reveal format/model defects, but do not establish real parameters or mechanisms. The original calls it ‘a wind tunnel’, a metaphor separating testing from external empirical support, not an actual wind-tunnel experiment. [T-main]","TOOL-AI-BOUNDARY requires disclosure of sources, material gaps, human responsible parties, uncertainty, prohibited uses, and review/withdrawal. It cannot cleanse unqualified content with a disclaimer: body text and actual use must respect ceilings. Responsibilities follow division of labor rather than assigning everything to the last person who clicks. [T-main; T-AI]","Automatic validation suits closed fields, resolvable references, consistent versions/states, and recalculation; it cannot replace material authentication, affected-party testimony, causal identification, or value choice. An openable source path establishes at most accessibility; source support and factual grounds still need checks. Logs may remain separately locatable from the text so readers see decisive reasons, not only pass counts. [T-main]","The Knight order adds a distinction about what certification wording claims: a process’s existence differs from controls meeting requirements. Text inspection may locate a statement difference, while actual control quality needs further material. This does not mean a regex can certify compliance or complete logs eliminate risk. [P-SEC]","The strongest objection is duplicated work across six tools. Shared record IDs can avoid copying the body six times, but circular self-certification is forbidden. If a ledger cites only a validation form and that form cites only the ledger, the closed loop adds no empirical support without external material. Reuse preserves dependencies and copying adds no independence; tools expose gaps rather than eliminate their names. [T-main]"],"argumentSteps":["Choose necessary tools for the task, stating each minimum product and ceiling.","The ledger preserves source-to-claim support chains; open assertions retain temporary status.","Assertion registration does not replace claim validation; failed foresight writes back to the original version.","Stress tests and AI boundaries expose failure conditions without using internal processes to certify reality.","Programs check form and arithmetic; people check material/testimony/causation/value; cross-tool references cannot self-certify circularly."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Source accessible","leftText":"A file or page opens.","rightTitle":"Claim supported","rightText":"Content and methods sufficiently support this sentence and use."},{"leftTitle":"Internal stress test","leftText":"Finds failure within a specified construction.","rightTitle":"External empirical support","rightText":"Actual object material and qualified comparisons."},{"leftTitle":"Reference reuse","leftText":"One source dependency retained for the same material.","rightTitle":"Independent replication","rightText":"Different acquisition processes support the same claim; multiple links cannot fabricate this."}],"boundaries":["Boundary in the original text: open assertions neither permit high-responsibility action nor replace claim validation.","Boundary in the original text: failed forecasts must write back; internal stress tests are not external empirical support.","Boundary in the original text: automatic validation does not replace authentication, testimony, causation, or value choice."],"questions":["Do two tools citing one report constitute two independent evidence sources?","Do passed stress tests establish real-world model effectiveness?","May a failed forecast’s original record be overwritten with a new target?","Does valid JSON certify author understanding and facts?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-13-7/figures/tools.png","caseLinks":[{"title":"Does certification concern ‘a process exists’ or ‘controls meet requirements’?","href":"https://www.sec.gov/files/litigation/admin/2013/34-70694.pdf"}],"caseNotes":[{"title":"Does certification concern ‘a process exists’ or ‘controls meet requirements’?","text":"Paragraphs 34 and 45 of the SEC’s 2013 Knight order distinguish certification of process existence from compliance of controls/procedures and identify errors in the relevant wording.","sourceLabel":"SEC：Knight Capital Americas LLC，Release 70694","sourceUrl":"https://www.sec.gov/files/litigation/admin/2013/34-70694.pdf","sourceLocator":"2013-10-16, p. 1; p. 10, ¶34; p. 13, ¶45.","boundary":"Shows only that the statement’s object and substantive examination are separate; programmatic detection of wording differences cannot independently decide actual compliance.","href":"https://www.sec.gov/files/litigation/admin/2013/34-70694.pdf"}],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"13.7","start_line":2915,"end_line":2929,"startLine":2915,"endLine":2929}],"outputId":"section-13-7","sourceHash":"51795bb7988f2669a324a52666c320cfba1382f597f7b5c6a1431844572da1fb","teachingExamples":[{"title":"Teaching construction: circular references add no support","text":"Artificial record E says ‘Judgment Q is grounded; see V’, while V says ‘Q is validated; see E’. Dependency graph E→V→E closes, but no edge reaches independent material S. Even if both files exist with valid fields, no external support for Q is supplied. Adding S later still requires sentence-level support checks, not merely another link.","assumptions":"All records are artificial; arrows indicate citation dependency, not causation.","boundary":"A logical structure example, not a denial that actual systems may use cyclic data structures; the restriction targets circular self-certification."},{"title":"Mathematical teaching construction: correct recalculation may not answer the question","text":"An artificial report says ‘2+3=5, so this project should receive priority resources’. A program can verify the arithmetic; the latter claim needs targets, comparisons, affected people, and normative premises and does not follow from addition. If 2 and 3 come from unverified records, correct arithmetic also does not establish actual total 5.","assumptions":"Numbers and project are artificial; no actual resources were allocated.","boundary":"Review formal validity, premise truth, and normative conclusions separately."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Boundary in the original text: open assertions neither permit high-responsibility action nor replace claim validation.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Boundary in the original text: failed forecasts must write back; internal stress tests are not external empirical support.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Boundary in the original text: automatic validation does not replace authentication, testimony, causation, or value choice.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"Do two tools citing one report constitute two independent evidence sources?","answer":"No. Retain the shared-source dependency.","basis":"Tools cannot circularly or repeatedly self-certify."},{"question":"Do passed stress tests establish real-world model effectiveness?","answer":"No. It supports only no detected failure of that type under tested conditions; external effectiveness needs separate testing.","basis":"Internal-testing boundary."},{"question":"May a failed forecast’s original record be overwritten with a new target?","answer":"No. Retain the original version and failure; revise in a new version and write back to related claims.","basis":"Foresight registry."},{"question":"Does valid JSON certify author understanding and facts?","answer":"No certification; only structural requirements are met.","basis":"Automatic-validation responsibility."}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-13-7/figures/tools.png","alt":"Six tools record the ledger, assertions, validation, foresight, stress tests, and AI boundaries separately.","caption":"Based on §13.7, without implying six software tools are implemented."},{"path":"/reading-assets/S-13-7/figures/cycle.png","alt":"E and V cite one another without independent material input; passing fields does not support the claim.","caption":"Artificial citation graph; arrows are not actual causation."}],"diagramBrief":"One static six-tool responsibility diagram and one circular-reference diagram. Future interaction could trace edges to external sources; absent edges should show gaps, not generate credibility from loop counts.","applicability":{"realCase":"applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"This unit concerns methodological judgment. Actual sources support only the listed facts; the diagram and teaching construction each retain their own status."},"openQuestions":["No six-tool software package was released and no actual foresight conducted; teaching diagrams and records are conceptual illustrations only."],"taskId":"S-13-7","sources":[{"id":"T-main","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§13.7, Chinese source lines 2779–2793","claim_support":["Six-tool responsibilities, no self-certification through mutual references, and automatic-validation boundaries."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"T-AI","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§13.8, Chinese source lines 2794–2801","claim_support":["AI-output disclosures and responsibility division."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"T-dynamic","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§13.10, Chinese source lines 2814–2831","claim_support":["Failed DF6 permits scenarios only; DF9 preserves original runs."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"P-SEC","title":"SEC：Knight Capital Americas LLC，Release 70694","url":"https://www.sec.gov/files/litigation/admin/2013/34-70694.pdf","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"2013-10-16","locator":"2013-10-16, p. 1; p. 10, ¶34; p. 13, ¶45.","claim_support":["The order distinguishes certification of a compliance process’s existence from certification that controls/procedures meet requirements and identifies problems in the certification text."],"limits":["A regulatory settlement finding; it does not establish machines’ independent ability to judge compliance or facts, or sufficiency of formal checks to prevent accidents."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"theory-v2","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v2, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v2 / 2026-11-16","locator":"13.7 Six Formal Tools; Chinese source lines 2835–2849; SHA-256 6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v2; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-13-7/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-13-7/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-13-7/source/S-13-7.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-13-7/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text and section locations correspond to v2; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"f536c54707bf208a66aa56d2ad4502b838c04cb65205ad06489b45cac5fa2550","source_sha256":"6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c"}},"13.8":{"title":"13.8 Boundaries on AI Use","lead":"AI may produce complete analyses, recommendations, and plans, while making material, inference, responsibility, and use explicit at every step. Generating text is neither consenting for people nor gaining authority to act in reality.","explanation":["Source location: Chinese source lines 2850–2857, original-site location /theory/part-13#section-13-8. The current full text is in the theory-v2 source; the historical research draft and excerpts remain downloadable in their original versions.","The original does not limit AI to copying tables and checking formats. It may organize material, check fields, compare versions, generate questions, compare mechanisms, list alternatives, and, where evidence and normative premises permit, form refutable judgments, recommend options, and draft complete institutional plans and commentary. Boundaries come from material and use, not permission to output only blank templates or never conclude. [T-main]","Each prohibition targets a different overreach: fabricated sources undermine traceability; training memory treated as current evidence confuses versions and timeliness; consenting for people or equating silence with consent removes their expressive position; replacing decision or authorization bodies confuses advice with authority; hidden uncertainty expands judgment ceilings; independent high-risk real decisions cross protection and execution responsibilities. AI’s usefulness cannot erase these differences, nor does risk justify rejecting every low-impact analysis. [T-main]","Each output should identify used material, gaps, observation/inference/analogy, final judgment responsibility, permitted uses, and review/withdrawal timing. Not every process log must enter body text, but key reasons and limits must be visible and details locatable. A final ‘for reference only’ does not repair unsupported definite accusations in the body or actual high-impact use. [T-main; T-tools]","Separate protective omission from absent evidence. Identity exposure, retaliation risk, or justified opacity may require withholding details; a public version should safely mark limits rather than fill protected positions as nonexistent. Conversely, ‘privacy’ cannot universally shield uncheckable claims; appropriate controlled verification routes and conclusion ceilings are needed. [T-main; T-missing]","Responsibility falls, according to the actual division of work, on material providers, those who determine goals and authority, system maintainers, executors, and reviewers. Technical facilities and programs do not become subjects capable of bearing moral or legal responsibility simply by producing recommendations or performing actions; organizations with particular institutional status must be assessed separately for the matter concerned. Neither anthropomorphize programs nor infer from this that organizations bear no responsibility. [T-main]","“A human clicks at the end” is not enough to establish effective review. Reviewers must have sufficient materials, time, capability, and stopping authority that can actually take effect; organizations must provide resources. If workloads exceed checking capacity, the system withholds original grounds, or irreversible action has already occurred by the time of the click, nominal human involvement cannot eliminate these breaks. Nor does this concentrate all responsibility on that reviewer. [T-main]","The 2024 NIST document on generative AI risks also sets out the division of roles, documentation, feedback and remedies, and monitoring and escalation as suggested actions, and discusses confabulation risks. This is an actual example of a public governance text, illustrating that review requires arrangements rather than a slogan. This lesson does not treat the document as an effectiveness trial or certification of any AI product’s accuracy. [P-NIST]","The strongest objection worries that responsibility for materials prevents AI from producing substantive content. The appropriate approach is to state supported bounded conclusions, complete feasible analysis and full drafts, and identify key gaps and conditions that could change the judgment; when real action is involved, proceed under valid authority. Protective boundaries should improve the quality of texts and proposals, not become an excuse to repeat disclaimers without completing the requested work. [T-main]"],"argumentSteps":["Determine the requested content deliverable and its actual use; distinguish analysis, recommendations, and drafts from real decisions.","Use traceable materials; label observations, inferences, analogies, and gaps. Do not present training memory as current verification.","When evidence and normative premises support them, provide complete bounded judgments and texts, not merely frameworks.","Record responsibilities according to the division of work; review requires materials, time, capability, and effective stopping authority.","List protective omissions separately; specify prohibited uses and the practical feasibility of review and withdrawal. Do not consent or authorize on others’ behalf."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Generative capability","leftText":"The ability to produce analysis, recommendations, or complete texts.","rightTitle":"Real-world authority","rightText":"Requires valid grounds corresponding to the subject, matter, scope, and period concerned."},{"leftTitle":"A human click","leftText":"A person participates through the interface.","rightTitle":"Effective review","rightText":"The person can actually understand, check, and stop the process in time."},{"leftTitle":"Withheld for protection","leftText":"Details do not appear in the public version.","rightTitle":"No evidence / nonexistence","rightText":"A different epistemic or factual state, which cannot automatically be equated with omission."}],"boundaries":["Boundary in the original: increased automation does not place all responsibility on the last person who clicks.","Boundary in the original: technical facilities do not become subjects of moral or legal responsibility by producing outputs or executing actions; organizations are assessed separately according to institutional status.","Teaching counterexample: giving a reviewer only two seconds and no original materials does not make substantive review complete merely because there is a confirmation button."],"questions":["If AI identifies differences between two proposals, may it make an explicit recommendation?","After removing protected persons’ names, may one write “no one objects”?","Does the last person who clicks bear all responsibility?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-13-8/figures/ai-boundary.png","caseLinks":[{"title":"Division of responsibilities and feedback in NIST’s 2024 generative AI governance text","href":"https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.600-1.pdf"}],"caseNotes":[{"title":"Division of responsibilities and feedback in NIST’s 2024 generative AI governance text","text":"NIST AI600-1 lists recommendations for roles and responsibilities, independent evaluation, and user feedback and remedies under GOVERN3.2; its monitoring provisions call for effective evaluation and escalation of issues.","sourceLabel":"NIST AI 600-1：Artificial Intelligence Risk Management Framework: Generative Artificial Intelligence Profile","sourceUrl":"https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.600-1.pdf","sourceLocator":"2024 edition; printed pages 1, Introduction; 6, §2.2; 18, GOVERN3.2; 35, MS-2.9-002; and 45, MG-4.1-007 and MG-4.3-001—002; PDF pages 5, 10, 22, 39, 49.","boundary":"These are recommendations in that edition, not procedures already implemented, demonstrated net effects, or binding regulations.","href":"https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.600-1.pdf"}],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"13.8","start_line":2930,"end_line":2937,"startLine":2930,"endLine":2937}],"outputId":"section-13-8","sourceHash":"51795bb7988f2669a324a52666c320cfba1382f597f7b5c6a1431844572da1fb","teachingExamples":[{"title":"Teaching construction: a review-capacity calculation","text":"A human team must review 120 AI summaries per day, with at least 5 minutes per summary required to check original materials: total demand is 600 minutes. One reviewer has 360 working minutes available. Without parallel assistance or a reduced workload, the shortfall is 240 minutes; at most 72 summaries can be reviewed to that standard. Conditional proposals may add resources, limit use, or adjust the workflow, but 120 button clicks cannot count as five minutes of verification for every summary.","assumptions":"Times and staffing are constructed; reviews are sequential and each requires 5 minutes, excluding other work. This is not an actual productivity study.","boundary":"The capacity calculation does not judge whether real employees failed in their duties, nor authorize forced overtime or reductions in necessary review."},{"title":"Teaching construction: keeping a complete draft separate from authorization","text":"The constructed request is to draft a procedure for supplementing evidence. AI can provide complete provisions on submission scope, recipients, evidence access, deadlines, protection against retaliation, version synchronization, and withdrawal conditions, while marking resources and authority still to be decided. The draft can be reviewed and used, but “the draft is complete” does not mean the organization has adopted it, assigned authority, or obtained the parties’ consent.","assumptions":"A constructed institution; no actual publication or configuration.","boundary":"Complete delivery and actual adoption are different endpoints; boundaries do not justify omitting requested draft content."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Boundary in the original: increased automation does not place all responsibility on the last person who clicks.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Boundary in the original: technical facilities do not become subjects of moral or legal responsibility by producing outputs or executing actions; organizations are assessed separately according to institutional status.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Teaching counterexample: giving a reviewer only two seconds and no original materials does not make substantive review complete merely because there is a confirmation button.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"If AI identifies differences between two proposals, may it make an explicit recommendation?","answer":"It may give grounded recommendations within the limits permitted by evidence and explicit normative premises, explaining the limitations; this does not permit it to execute high-risk decisions on its own.","basis":"The original permits substantive analysis and recommendations."},{"question":"After removing protected persons’ names, may one write “no one objects”?","answer":"No. Protective omission is not equivalent to absence of objection.","basis":"Protective status."},{"question":"Does the last person who clicks bear all responsibility?","answer":"No. Assess responsibilities separately according to the actual division of work in materials, goals and authority, maintenance, execution, and review.","basis":"The original text's final paragraph."}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-13-8/figures/ai-boundary.png","alt":"AI may produce substantive content, but must disclose key grounds and limits and cannot consent or authorize on others’ behalf.","caption":"Based on §13.8; not certification of any product’s capability or compliance."},{"path":"/reading-assets/S-13-8/figures/review-capacity.png","alt":"In the constructed example, 120 summaries × 5 minutes require 600 minutes; 360 available minutes permit at most 72 summaries, leaving a 240-minute shortfall.","caption":"A capacity teaching example only, with no real productivity data."}],"diagramBrief":"Two static diagrams: three layers of capability, evidence, and use; and five types of necessary conditions for reviewers, with the capacity calculation. Future interaction could adjust available time to display constraints, without treating click counts as review quality.","applicability":{"realCase":"applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"This unit concerns methodological judgment. Actual sources support only the listed facts; the diagram and teaching construction each retain their own status."},"openQuestions":["No performance of a particular AI system, capability of real reviewers, or institutional authority and responsibility was evaluated; no high-risk real-world decisions were executed."],"taskId":"S-13-8","sources":[{"id":"T-main","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§13.8, Chinese source lines 2794–2801","claim_support":["Permitted AI uses, prohibitions, disclosure, protective status, division of responsibilities, and review resources."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"T-tools","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§13.7, Chinese source lines 2779–2793","claim_support":["Separate, locatable logs and main text; boundaries of formal checks."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"T-missing","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§5.8, Chinese source lines 1221–1228","claim_support":["The four missingness states cannot be interpreted as nonexistence."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"P-NIST","title":"NIST AI 600-1：Artificial Intelligence Risk Management Framework: Generative Artificial Intelligence Profile","url":"https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.600-1.pdf","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"NIST AI600-1，2024","locator":"2024 edition; printed pages 1, Introduction; 6, §2.2; 18, GOVERN3.2; 35, MS-2.9-002; and 45, MG-4.1-007 and MG-4.3-001—002; PDF pages 5, 10, 22, 39, 49.","claim_support":["The document presents itself as a companion resource to the AI RMF and provides suggested actions; it lists confabulation risks and requirements concerning roles, documentation, feedback and remedies, and monitoring and escalation."],"limits":["Only that edition’s governance recommendations are compared here. This does not demonstrate implementation or effectiveness, nor equate a 2024 document with current binding law or certification of a product’s performance."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"theory-v2","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v2, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v2 / 2026-11-16","locator":"13.8 AI use boundaries; Chinese source lines 2850–2857; SHA-256 6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v2; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-13-8/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-13-8/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-13-8/source/S-13-8.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-13-8/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text and section locations correspond to v2; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"d6cf010815d2dfadd8b4919d9462974d5afdcf53f9c0dbb44b769f74472c2e8c","source_sha256":"6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c"}},"13.9":{"title":"13.9 Execution records and failure semantics","lead":"Execution records let readers see what passed, what failed, what was not run, and the maximum permissible use of a report. They are auditable records of judgment and handoff, not automatically generated licenses.","explanation":["Source location: Chinese source lines 2858–2869; original site location /theory/part-13#section-13-9. See the theory-v2 source for the current full text; historical research drafts and excerpts remain available for checking in their original downloadable versions.","“Field closure” in flow_execution_record means that every dimension required for recording has an explicit place; ad hoc notes cannot bypass statuses and ceilings. Object and scale, L1–L3, IT1–IT4, IG1–IG7, diagnostic elaboration and SJ triggering, as well as evidence, alternative explanations, uncertainty, categories, judgment/action ceilings, normative handoff, appeals, rollback, and statuses must all be checkable. Complete fields establish only formal completeness; claims within them still require corresponding materials. [T-main]","The three closed output ceilings are description_only, diagnostic_only, and requirements_only, bounded respectively by description, diagnosis, and requirements. The specific categories allowed at each level must be checked against the fixed rules of the implementation used; callers cannot ad hoc add “execution instructions.” All three fix can_authorize=false and can_execute=false. This section of the original does not enumerate each level’s full category set; this explanation does not invent an official enumeration and present it as the original. [T-main]","action_requirements states conditions that external choice and authorization procedures must satisfy, such as required evidence, protections, responsibilities, and stopping paths. It has no execution effect. Even if free text in a report says “authorized,” it cannot alter the closed Boolean values, still less permit downstream systems to read only that sentence while ignoring authority fields. [T-main；T-seven]","Normative handoff should link to verifiable external choice, C12, and authorization records, or explicitly identify what is missing. Object, action, scope, time, and actual verification grounds must match the current use. “The committee approved it” does not identify which object, when, or which actions were approved; circularly citing this record’s own “verified” status adds no external grounds. Formats may vary across implementations; verification responsibility does not. [T-main]","Statuses first distinguish execution facts: passed means the gate in that record passed, not that a proposition about the world is true; failed means it was actually run and did not pass; not_run means an earlier gate or the scope prevented it from running. When an earlier gate fails, later dependent steps cannot pretend to have been checked; failed especially cannot be rewritten as not_applicable to erase failure. Another judgment with independent grounds still requires independent review; one cannot copy the failed chain and revive it under a new name. [T-main；T-seven；T-IT]","The four missingness states answer different questions: unknown means currently unknown; not_applicable means inapplicable for a stated reason; not_observable means currently unobservable; withheld_for_protection means withheld for protection. These are neither a bad-to-good ranking nor four aliases for a null value. Inability to disclose protected materials does not mean the matter does not exist; protective status cannot automatically be recorded as “nonexistent.” [T-main；T-missing]","authorization_effect is always none. Even with complete external normative choice, C12, J, and O records, the tool only checks and hands off existing grounds. Valid external authority may exist, but this record is not its source. Normative legitimacy, authority scope, execution resources, and stopping conditions still require separate checks. The record’s lack of authorizing effect does not negate genuinely established external authorization; conversely, an external label cannot make this record issue authorization itself. [T-main]","Reviewed outputs must also retain accessible appeals and practical withdrawal/correction paths. Tool conclusions that cannot be withdrawn must not enter high-impact uses that are difficult to reverse. Withdrawal involves more than changing the report title: it must allow correction of records and uses that actually adopted the conclusion. Where consequences already incurred cannot be fully reversed, residual effects must be stated and remedial needs proposed. The strongest objection says rigorous records are cumbersome; the appropriate response is concise main text with locatable details, not deletion of failures or authority checks. [T-main；T-tools；T-endpoint]","Paragraph 51M of the SEC Knight order requires narrative and attachments to support certification of compliance and allows requests for further evidence. This concretely illustrates the difference between “claimed complete” and “checkable grounds.” However, the order specifies external regulatory requirements; it is not an instance of this lesson’s record, and its text alone cannot establish actual completion. The case serves only this local comparison of auditable requirements. [P-SEC]"],"argumentSteps":["Fix the object, scale, use, and level; record conversions, gates, and applicable procedures item by item.","Match each result to its actual execution or missingness status; mark subsequent steps dependent on failure as not_run.","Narrow ceilings according to fixed output categories; authorization and execution Booleans are false at all three levels.","Check external records’ object, action, scope, time, and grounds; explicitly record any missing elements.","Keep authorization_effect as none; provide actually accessible appeals, correction, and withdrawal paths."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"passed","leftText":"The record gate’s requirements have been satisfied.","rightTitle":"Fact true","rightText":"Still requires specific evidence and support from the proposition’s contract."},{"leftTitle":"failed","leftText":"Run and failed.","rightTitle":"not_run","rightText":"Not run; one cannot say it was reviewed and failed, or reviewed and passed."},{"leftTitle":"withheld_for_protection","leftText":"Not disclosed for protection.","rightTitle":"not_observable","rightText":"Currently unavailable to observation; the cause and handling differ."},{"leftTitle":"Checking external authorization","leftText":"Checking existing records and their scope.","rightTitle":"Issuing authorization","rightText":"This tool record has no such effect."}],"boundaries":["Boundary in the original: every output ceiling has can_authorize=false and can_execute=false, and authorization_effect is always none.","Boundary in the original: failure cannot be changed to inapplicability, and protective withholding cannot be changed to nonexistence.","Teaching counterexample: a workflow form with text in every field still supplies neither independent evidence nor external authority if its sole ground is itself."],"questions":["If failure at an earlier gate means a later gate did not run, should the later gate be marked failed?","Once external authorization is valid, can this record’s authorization_effect change?","May an identity withheld for protection be recorded as indicating that no such person exists?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-13-9/figures/states.png","caseLinks":[{"title":"A regulatory order requires evidence supporting completion claims","href":"https://www.sec.gov/files/litigation/admin/2013/34-70694.pdf"}],"caseNotes":[{"title":"A regulatory order requires evidence supporting completion claims","text":"Paragraph 51M of the SEC’s 2013 Knight order requires certification of compliance to identify the undertakings, support them with narrative and attachments, and permit further requests for evidence.","sourceLabel":"SEC Knight administrative settlement order: certification-of-compliance requirements","sourceUrl":"https://www.sec.gov/files/litigation/admin/2013/34-70694.pdf","sourceLocator":"2013-10-16; PDF page 1 and page 17, ¶51M.","boundary":"Establishes only that the order imposed this requirement, not that actual remediation was completed; the order is not transferred into authority for this lesson.","href":"https://www.sec.gov/files/litigation/admin/2013/34-70694.pdf"}],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"13.9","start_line":2938,"end_line":2949,"startLine":2938,"endLine":2949}],"outputId":"section-13-9","sourceHash":"51795bb7988f2669a324a52666c320cfba1382f597f7b5c6a1431844572da1fb","teachingExamples":[{"title":"Teaching construction: a local record of failure and nonexecution","text":"For constructed proposition Q, IT1 obtains a signal; IT2 is marked failed because source status cannot be verified, and dependent IT3/IT4 are marked not_run. The record may still describe the obtained materials whose status is unverified and the gaps, but cannot form the strong judgment originally dependent on that source. If another proposition R relies on independently verifiable sources, its evidence and use must be listed separately; R’s passing cannot be used to rewrite Q.","assumptions":"A constructed proposition and local field display, not a claim to have completed a real full-process record.","boundary":"If the status gap affects a shared foundation, R is also blocked; independence must have grounds."},{"title":"Teaching construction: conflict between authority fields and free text","text":"A constructed record has output_ceiling=requirements_only, can_authorize=false, can_execute=false, and authorization_effect=none. Its author writes in a note, “All authority has been obtained; execute immediately.” The note conflicts with the ceiling and should be removed or revised into the required external verification conditions. If a separate record A exists, cite it only when object, action, scope, period, and grounds match; false cannot be changed to true.","assumptions":"The field name output_ceiling is used for this teaching illustration; this does not claim that the original specifies every implementation key name.","boundary":"This example involves no real authorization or execution, and does not infer that external record A actually exists."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Boundary in the original: every output ceiling has can_authorize=false and can_execute=false, and authorization_effect is always none.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Boundary in the original: failure cannot be changed to inapplicability, and protective withholding cannot be changed to nonexistence.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Teaching counterexample: a workflow form with text in every field still supplies neither independent evidence nor external authority if its sole ground is itself.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"If failure at an earlier gate means a later gate did not run, should the later gate be marked failed?","answer":"Mark the later gate not_run and state the dependency reason; failed applies to the item that was run and failed.","basis":"Execution and result differ."},{"question":"Once external authorization is valid, can this record’s authorization_effect change?","answer":"No. It remains none; the record checks and hands off external authorization.","basis":"A constant boundary in the original."},{"question":"May an identity withheld for protection be recorded as indicating that no such person exists?","answer":"No. Retain the protective-withholding status and a safe verification path.","basis":"The four missingness states."}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-13-9/figures/states.png","alt":"Separate execution passing, execution failure, nonexecution, and the four missingness states.","caption":"Statuses are not interchangeable; passed does not automatically certify facts about the world."},{"path":"/reading-assets/S-13-9/figures/ceiling.png","alt":"None of the three ceilings authorizes or executes; external references permit only verification and handoff.","caption":"Based on §13.9; this diagram is not an authorization credential."}],"diagramBrief":"Two static diagrams show status distinctions and closed ceilings respectively. Future interaction could highlight dependent not_run steps when a failed gate is clicked; it cannot automatically grant authority.","applicability":{"realCase":"applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"This unit concerns methodological judgment. Actual sources support only the listed facts; the diagram and teaching construction each retain their own status."},"openQuestions":["No real flow_execution_record system was built or run. A particular implementation’s fixed output-category table must be checked separately; no claim is made that this section already contains a complete enumeration."],"taskId":"S-13-9","sources":[{"id":"T-main","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§13.9, Chinese source lines 2802–2813","claim_support":["Closed records, three ceilings, external verification, statuses, absence of authorizing effect, and correction."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"T-seven","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§13.3, Chinese source lines 2702–2719","claim_support":["Failure dependencies, independent judgments, IG6 applicability, and why the seven gates do not prove truth."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"T-IT","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§13.1, Chinese source lines 2681–2695","claim_support":["Failure-dependency boundaries of the four conversions."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"T-missing","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§5.8, Chinese source lines 1221–1228","claim_support":["The four missingness states."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"T-tools","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§13.7, Chinese source lines 2779–2793","claim_support":["Logs and clear main text can be located separately."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"T-endpoint","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§13.11.2, Chinese source lines 2846–2863","claim_support":["Actual endpoints and consequences of correction."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"P-SEC","title":"SEC Knight administrative settlement order: certification-of-compliance requirements","url":"https://www.sec.gov/files/litigation/admin/2013/34-70694.pdf","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"2013-10-16","locator":"2013-10-16; PDF page 1 and page 17, ¶51M.","claim_support":["Paragraph 51M requires written identification of undertakings, narrative evidence of compliance, and sufficient attachments; staff may request further evidence."],"limits":["This is a regulatory order’s requirement, not proof that undertakings were fulfilled. The historical order is not current legal advice for an individual case or a source of Xikari authorization."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"theory-v2","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v2, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v2 / 2026-11-16","locator":"13.9 Execution records and failure semantics; Chinese source lines 2858–2869; SHA-256 6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v2; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-13-9/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-13-9/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-13-9/source/S-13-9.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-13-9/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text and section locations correspond to v2; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"e1fa4bf1dcd20317d9b6ae3ccf6962c324c10ac890090875c6f3425b6c0d234a","source_sha256":"6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c"}},"13.10":{"title":"13.10 Nine gates for multi-circle projection","lead":"The nine gates dynamically elaborate on the seven gates: from freezing facts through branching, foresight, choice, handoff, and write-back. They make conditional paths open to questioning, but cannot turn conditional stories into validated predictions or permission to execute.","explanation":["Source location: Chinese source lines 2870–2887; original site location /theory/part-13#section-13-10. See the theory-v2 source for the current full text; historical research drafts and excerpts remain available for checking in their original downloadable versions.","The seven gates remain the sole main process for explanation and diagnosis. DF1–DF9 are expanded only when the task explicitly requires dynamic projection, conditional foresight, or bounded choice. Ordinary descriptions need not run all nine gates merely to appear comprehensive, nor may the nine gates bypass objects, evidence, affected parties, power, reflexivity, strong judgments, or action ceilings. Every output dependent on upstream failure remains constrained by the main process. [T-main；T-seven]","DF1, fact freezing, first fixes event types, sources, times, the evidence cutoff, and disputes. It freezes the materials available and the judgment version for this run, not a claim that disputes have been settled; later evidence may open a new version. If it fails, provide a list of factual questions to avoid retrospectively rewriting what was originally known after learning the outcome. DF2, the joint object, then checks actors, circles, relations, members, and K; if it fails, retain only candidate groupings or return to a single focus. Lines on a diagram do not create membership binding on other people. [T-main]","DF3, dual channels and clocks, records only task-relevant material and experiential–meaning conditions, their corresponding times, and unknowns. Irrelevant items may be marked inapplicable with reasons; not every task mandates investigating inner experience. Where conditions are missing, retain the snapshot and pause propagation dependent on them, reviewing genuinely independent paths separately. Entering 0 for “unknown experience” replaces missingness with a measurement. A shared date should not conceal different update cycles across multiple clocks. [T-main；T-clock]","DF4 mechanism propagation requires channels, thresholds, delays, feedback, and cascades; drawing arrows between correlated variables does not prove causation. On failure, list candidate mechanisms and observations that could distinguish them. DF5 path branching links conditions, parent/child nodes, and early and contrary signals. Where conditions are insufficiently distinguished, retain parallel paths without forced rankings or invented probabilities. Tool-proposed DF2 circles and DF5 variables remain candidate and cannot automatically upgrade without external evidence. [T-main]","DF6 forecast registration must specify targets, deadlines, simple baselines, calibration, and write-back. Without these, the output is only scenario projection and must not masquerade as prediction through “will” or “expected hit” wording. Registration does not guarantee a hit: outcomes still require comparison under prespecified windows and metrics. One smaller error establishes neither cross-context calibration nor stable gain; failures also enter the record. [T-main; T-tools]","DF7 normative choice checks N, PF, affected people, options, and inaction. Pause recommendations specifically blocked by gaps while retaining independently grounded evaluations, conditional advice with explicit premises, and evidence needs. Do not erase all factual analysis or smuggle in a premise-free uniquely optimal option. Predictive accuracy alone cannot justify choice; a low-error option may still breach the protection floor. [T-main]","DF8 authorization and execution checks J, O, responsibilities, stopping, rollback, and remedies; failure means external handoff without execution. Failed DF7 or DF8 cannot produce executable instructions. Even with conditions complete, §13.9 prohibits the tool from generating authorization; actual execution occurs under valid external permissions and procedures. Action reversibility must be realized in the relevant system; the words “can roll back” are not an operational safeguard. [T-main; T-record]","DF9 outcome write-back records outcomes, baseline comparisons, deviations, downgrading, and retirement. It must preserve failures and open new versions rather than overwrite the original run with corrected normal output. A path weakened by contrary signals should be narrowed or retired, not reinterpret every outcome as “anticipated all along.” New versions may still repair it; one failure is not permanent inability to learn. [T-main]","The Knight order records inconsistent deployment across eight servers, leaving old and new processing paths in parallel, followed by code removal that worsened the problem. This helps locate state, branching, and response-feedback checks, but our reading is retrospective: it cannot backfill DF6 forecasts for participants or prove the nine gates could prevent the accident. The case provides only bounded teaching comparisons against verified historical paths. [P-SEC]","The strongest objection is that nine gates turn analysis into lengthy procedure. The answer is not mechanically filling nine boxes: enable dynamic expansion according to the task, using conditions that actually affect judgment. Explain known non-applicability, preserve unknowns, and deliver explicit conditional branches for pure scenarios. Complexity must yield reviewable boundaries rather than procedural names replacing substantive explanation. [T-main]"],"argumentSteps":["First preserve the seven-gate main process and the current object's evidence and use, confirming that the task needs dynamic expansion.","DF1 freezes material; DF2 checks joint objects; DF3 distinguishes relevant dual channels and clocks.","DF4 establishes testable candidate mechanisms; DF5 retains conditional branches and early/contrary signals.","Without complete foresight conditions, DF6 remains a scenario; registration still requires baseline comparison and write-back.","DF7 independently checks values and protection; DF8 checks external permissions and execution conditions, issuing no instructions on failure.","DF9 preserves original runs, outcomes, and failures, opens new versions, and permits downgrading or retirement."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Scenario projection","leftText":"Develops possible paths under explicit conditions.","rightTitle":"Registered foresight","rightText":"A predictive task with target, deadline, baseline, calibration, and write-back responsibilities."},{"leftTitle":"candidate","leftText":"Candidate circles or variables awaiting minimal tests.","rightTitle":"Established object/variable","rightText":"Requires corresponding contracts and external evidence; AI naming cannot create it."},{"leftTitle":"Path ranking","leftText":"Requires adequate comparative grounds.","rightTitle":"Parallel retention","rightText":"Preserves branches under insufficient grounds without inventing precise probabilities."},{"leftTitle":"Repair in a new version","leftText":"Preserves old failures and explains changes.","rightTitle":"Overwriting an old run","rightText":"Erases failure and violates DF9."}],"boundaries":["Original-text boundary: nine gates must not bypass the seven-gate main process or action ceilings.","Original-text boundary: failed DF6 permits only scenarios; failed DF7/DF8 permits no executable instructions.","Teaching counterexample: three detailed branches without a target window or baseline do not become a registered forecast."],"questions":["If DF6 fails but branches are complete, may an outcome be described as expected to occur inevitably?","If an experiential condition is unknown, must every count description be deleted?","If prediction fails, does changing the original prediction to the actual outcome count as learning?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-13-10/figures/df-start.png","caseLinks":[{"title":"Parallel code states and response feedback in the Knight incident","href":"https://www.sec.gov/files/litigation/admin/2013/34-70694.pdf"}],"caseNotes":[{"title":"Parallel code states and response feedback in the Knight incident","text":"The SEC order records one server missing new-code deployment and processing differently from seven correctly deployed servers; removing the new code from the seven during the incident worsened the problem.","sourceLabel":"SEC Knight order: erroneous code paths and response consequences","sourceUrl":"https://www.sec.gov/files/litigation/admin/2013/34-70694.pdf","sourceLocator":"2013-10-16, PDF p. 1, p. 6 ¶15–16, p. 8 ¶27.","boundary":"Historical material serves only as comparison for state/branch/feedback checks, not prior registration or proof of this framework's effectiveness.","href":"https://www.sec.gov/files/litigation/admin/2013/34-70694.pdf"}],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"13.10","start_line":2950,"end_line":2967,"startLine":2950,"endLine":2967}],"outputId":"section-13-10","sourceHash":"51795bb7988f2669a324a52666c320cfba1382f597f7b5c6a1431844572da1fb","teachingExamples":[{"title":"Teaching construction: conditional branching with stopping points","text":"An artificial service system analyzes only request congestion. Path A: increased arrival rate, with larger arrival counts as an early signal. Path B: reduced processing capacity, with lower completion counts as an early signal. A queue-length snapshot alone allows both explanations: DF4 retains candidates and DF5 parallel branches. Without a prior deadline and baseline, DF6 is scenario only; it cannot say “A will cause it.” If user experience is unsampled, mark unknown and pause only conclusions depending on experiential propagation; independent count checks remain possible.","assumptions":"An artificial system without actual operation, probability estimation, or empirical causal identification; experiential relevance depends on this lesson's use.","boundary":"No actual service object is established, and no adjustments or resource allocations are executed."},{"title":"Teaching construction: outcome write-back and a baseline","text":"An artificial calculation has original record r1 prespecify next-window completions of 7, simple baseline 6, and absolute error. Given teaching outcome 8, forecast error is |7−8|=1 and baseline error |6−8|=2. Error is lower by 1 at this single artificial point; this establishes neither good calibration nor stable predictive gain. Changing the forecast to 8 requires new version r2, not overwriting r1 to claim zero error.","assumptions":"All values and ordering are teaching assumptions, not actual preregistration or measurement; the calibration protocol needed for complete DF6 is not implemented here.","boundary":"Illustrates error and version logic only, without claiming the teaching record passed DF6 or has actual predictive validity."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Original-text boundary: nine gates must not bypass the seven-gate main process or action ceilings.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Original-text boundary: failed DF6 permits only scenarios; failed DF7/DF8 permits no executable instructions.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Teaching counterexample: three detailed branches without a target window or baseline do not become a registered forecast.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"If DF6 fails but branches are complete, may an outcome be described as expected to occur inevitably?","answer":"No. Give only conditional scenarios, with conditions and unknowns explicit.","basis":"Foresight responsibilities."},{"question":"If an experiential condition is unknown, must every count description be deleted?","answer":"No. Pause propagation depending on the missing condition; independently grounded count paths receive separate review.","basis":"DF3 dependency boundaries."},{"question":"If prediction fails, does changing the original prediction to the actual outcome count as learning?","answer":"It is not valid write-back. Preserve the old record and failure, then revise in a new version.","basis":"DF9 prohibits overwriting."}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-13-10/figures/df-start.png","alt":"DF1 factual freezing, DF2 joint objects, DF3 dual-channel clocks, with respective failure outputs.","caption":"The nine gates expand only on top of the seven gates."},{"path":"/reading-assets/S-13-10/figures/df-branch.png","alt":"DF4 mechanism propagation, DF5 conditional branching, DF6 forecast registration; without registration, scenario only.","caption":"Candidates and parallel branches do not automatically produce forecasts."},{"path":"/reading-assets/S-13-10/figures/df-return.png","alt":"DF7 normative choice, DF8 externally authorized execution, DF9 new versions preserving failures.","caption":"Conditional advice is not a command, and old records cannot be overwritten."}],"diagramBrief":"Three static diagrams show all nine gates and failure ceilings in DF1–3, DF4–6, and DF7–9 groups. Future interaction could toggle a failed gate to highlight blocked dependencies, without automatically assigning probabilities, normative priorities, or execution permissions.","applicability":{"realCase":"applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"This unit concerns methodological judgment. Actual sources support only the listed facts; the diagram and teaching construction each retain their own status."},"openQuestions":["No actual forecast registration, calibration experiment, or test of nine-gate governance effects was performed; actual circle and variable candidates require additional evidence."],"taskId":"S-13-10","sources":[{"id":"T-main","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§13.10, Chinese source lines 2814–2831","claim_support":["Complete DF1–9, triggers, failure ceilings, and candidate/version boundaries."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"T-seven","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§13.3, Chinese source lines 2702–2719","claim_support":["The seven gates as the sole main process and their dependencies."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"T-clock","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§10.7, Chinese source lines 2161–2176","claim_support":["Clocks registered separately."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"T-tools","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§13.7, Chinese source lines 2779–2793","claim_support":["Forecast registration and failure write-back."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"T-record","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§13.9, Chinese source lines 2802–2813","claim_support":["Fixed boundaries for tool authorization and execution."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"P-SEC","title":"SEC Knight order: erroneous code paths and response consequences","url":"https://www.sec.gov/files/litigation/admin/2013/34-70694.pdf","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"2013-10-16","locator":"2013-10-16, PDF p. 1, p. 6 ¶15–16, p. 8 ¶27.","claim_support":["One server omitted in deployment caused different old/new-code processing; removing new code from seven servers during response worsened the failure."],"limits":["Retrospective regulatory settlement material, not prior forecast registration or a nine-gate intervention trial. It does not establish that this framework would necessarily have prevented the accident."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"theory-v2","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v2, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v2 / 2026-11-16","locator":"13.10 Nine Gates for Multi-Circle Projection; Chinese source lines 2870–2887; SHA-256 6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v2; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-13-10/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-13-10/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-13-10/source/S-13-10.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-13-10/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text and section locations correspond to v2; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"01e9bde0aaade035c49d466ffdded0d8ba9b3bd69469d3327f36cb467530701e","source_sha256":"6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c"}},"13.11":{"title":"13.11 AI Boundaries in Dynamic Operation","lead":"AI can help clarify, calculate, and compare dynamic problems and write complete recommendations. Its generated classifications, simulations, and self-reports cannot alone certify world facts or determine other people's rights boundaries.","explanation":["Chinese source location: lines 2888–2891; original-site location /theory/part-13#section-13-11. See the theory-v2 source for the current full text; the historical research manuscript and excerpts remain downloadable in their original versions.","This section governs two elaborations: 13.11.1 separates evidence for material checking from tool actions, and 13.11.2 grounds task endpoints, concurrency, retries, and stopping conditions in checkable objects. Together they require AI to complete grounded substantive work without treating fluent text as proof that the external world changed. [T-main; T-check; T-end]","Permitted work includes organizing material, checking locatable original text, identifying missing fields, generating and comparing competing paths, checking contract consistency, and calculating registered metrics. Explicit normative premises also permit grounded local judgments, recommendations, and complete texts. Scope is not confined to questions or templates; conclusions must remain tied to supporting objects, information, periods, and uses. [T-main]","A model saying “I verified it,” internal scores, or repeatedly generating the same answer cannot alone certify actual events. External material is not uniformly trustworthy either; distinguish its status, methods, counterexamples, and uses. Faithfully reporting a source establishes reporting accuracy, not automatically the truth of that source's claims. Separating them allows precise statements about completed steps and remaining evidential gaps. [T-main; T-check]","Prohibitions protect facts, subjects, and permissions separately: do not diagnose personality from text, disclose information for others, impose binding circle boundaries through one's own classification, treat simulation as fact, determine disputed value premises for others, or issue authorization. Analyzing observable behavior differs from concluding personality; proposing candidate classifications differs from letting classifications determine treatment. These distinctions enable useful work within boundaries. [T-main]","When AI proposes variables automatically, record their model-candidate status, minimal tests, and prohibited uses. “Communication delay may explain request backlog,” for example, remains a candidate requiring observable timestamps, comparison conditions, and alternative mechanisms; it cannot directly become a capability score for a particular person. Without corresponding external evidence, retain candidate, regardless of attractive graphics or repeated generation. [T-main; T-dynamic]","The NIST 2024 document discusses erroneous generated content, including false logic or citations offered in support, and recommends responsibility arrangements. It is used here as an existing risk-governance text illustrating why evidence checks matter, not to estimate any product's current error rate or infer that all AI output is wrong because risk exists. [P-NIST]","The strongest objection is that AI's inability to certify independently would leave it unable to do anything. Locatable citations, transparent calculations, condition comparisons, and complete drafting are substantive outputs. Certification boundaries require accurate claims of completion, not denial of those outputs. Conversely, where actual external evidence and valid permissions are established, generic disclaimers should not obscure a supportable clear conclusion. [T-main]"],"argumentSteps":["Specify the dynamic task, object, material, and use, keeping normative premises explicit.","Complete locatable checks, candidate comparisons, registered-metric calculations, and required full texts.","Separate observations, model candidates, simulations, and empirical judgments, marking minimal tests and prohibited uses.","Do not disclose for others, settle disputed values, issue permissions, or let classifications determine others' eligibility.","Use 13.11.1 to check material and actions, and 13.11.2 to check corresponding task endpoints."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Model candidate","leftText":"A variable or path proposed by AI for testing.","rightTitle":"Empirical validity","rightText":"Requires appropriate external evidence and boundaries."},{"leftTitle":"Textual faithfulness","leftText":"Accurately reports the given source.","rightTitle":"Truth about the world","rightText":"The source's content itself still requires verification."},{"leftTitle":"Recommendation with explicit normative premises","leftText":"Conditions and reasons are open to challenge.","rightTitle":"Determining values for others","rightText":"Treating disputed premises as others' consent oversteps the boundary."}],"boundaries":["Original-text boundary: internal processes and model self-reports cannot alone certify actual events.","Original-text boundary: automatic variables retain candidate status and cannot upgrade without corresponding external evidence.","Teaching counterexample: an AI-generated membership diagram without participant-based grounds and relevant permissions cannot determine who is excluded."],"questions":["Can AI write a complete proposal rather than a blank framework?","Does proposing the same variable three times upgrade it to a real mechanism?","Does accurate quotation complete verification of world facts?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-13-11/figures/dynamic-ai.png","caseLinks":[{"title":"NIST's public discussion of confabulation risk","href":"https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.600-1.pdf"}],"caseNotes":[{"title":"NIST's public discussion of confabulation risk","text":"NIST AI 600-1 §2.2 discusses erroneous content and false logic/citations; GOVERN 3.2 lists recommendations on roles, responsibilities, feedback, and redress.","sourceLabel":"NIST AI 600-1: generative-AI risk document","sourceUrl":"https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.600-1.pdf","sourceLocator":"2024 edition; PDF p. 5/printed p. 1, Introduction; PDF p. 10/printed p. 6, §2.2 Confabulation; PDF p. 22/printed p. 18, GOVERN 3.2.","boundary":"Used as a 2024 risk-governance text, not experimental evidence of a model's current performance or this framework's effects.","href":"https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.600-1.pdf"}],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"13.11","start_line":2968,"end_line":2971,"startLine":2968,"endLine":2971}],"outputId":"section-13-11","sourceHash":"51795bb7988f2669a324a52666c320cfba1382f597f7b5c6a1431844572da1fb","teachingExamples":[{"title":"Teaching construction: from variable candidate to minimal test","text":"AI in an artificial system proposes V, “handoff delay,” to explain backlog. Register V as a model candidate; the test proposal checks receipt/handoff timestamps, removes duplicate records, and compares the competing explanation of changed workload. Prohibited uses include directly rating or punishing individual capability from V. Even a calculated mean delay yields only the corresponding description; causal explanation requires further discrimination.","assumptions":"Artificial objects, variables, and proposal; no actual employee data were collected or evaluations performed.","boundary":"A designed test is not a passed test, much less permission to change members' treatment."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Original-text boundary: internal processes and model self-reports cannot alone certify actual events.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Original-text boundary: automatic variables retain candidate status and cannot upgrade without corresponding external evidence.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Teaching counterexample: an AI-generated membership diagram without participant-based grounds and relevant permissions cannot determine who is excluded.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"Can AI write a complete proposal rather than a blank framework?","answer":"Yes. Within the material and explicit normative premises, write a complete proposal and explain gaps and uses.","basis":"The original permitted scope."},{"question":"Does proposing the same variable three times upgrade it to a real mechanism?","answer":"No. Repeated generation is not corresponding external evidence.","basis":"Candidate status."},{"question":"Does accurate quotation complete verification of world facts?","answer":"Not necessarily. The original source may itself be false.","basis":"Separate faithfulness from truth."}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-13-11/figures/dynamic-ai.png","alt":"AI may deliver complete content while variables remain candidates; self-report cannot decide facts, values, or authorization.","caption":"Based on §13.11; this does not refuse substantive analysis."}],"diagramBrief":"A static diagram separates deliverable work, candidate responsibilities, and matters AI cannot replace. Future interaction could show a candidate's minimal test and prohibited uses without automatic certification.","applicability":{"realCase":"applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"This unit concerns methodological judgment. Actual sources support only the listed facts; the diagram and teaching construction each retain their own status."},"openQuestions":["No model's current performance was measured; the teaching variable has no actual evidence and remains a candidate."],"taskId":"S-13-11","sources":[{"id":"T-main","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§13.11, Chinese source lines 2832–2835","claim_support":["AI dynamic work, prohibitions on overreach, and variable-candidate responsibilities."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"T-check","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§13.11.1, Chinese source lines 2836–2845","claim_support":["Four material checks, boundaries of retrieval and consistency, formal checking, and tool-action chains."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"T-end","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§13.11.2, Chinese source lines 2846–2863","claim_support":["Five endpoint levels, concurrency/timeouts, stopping, and failure preservation."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"T-dynamic","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§13.10, Chinese source lines 2814–2831","claim_support":["Dynamic candidates, conditional branches, and normative permissions."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"T-human","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§13.8, Chinese source lines 2794–2801","claim_support":["Resources and stopping permissions for effective human review."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"P-NIST","title":"NIST AI 600-1: generative-AI risk document","url":"https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.600-1.pdf","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"2024","locator":"2024 edition; PDF p. 5/printed p. 1, Introduction; PDF p. 10/printed p. 6, §2.2 Confabulation; PDF p. 22/printed p. 18, GOVERN 3.2.","claim_support":["This edition's voluntary companion document discusses confabulation, erroneous logic/citation risks, division of roles, and recommendations for feedback and redress."],"limits":["A governance text and risk discussion, not this lesson's experiment, a quantitative estimate of all models' performance, or current mandatory law."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"P-AWS","title":"Malcolm Featonby：Making retries safe with idempotent APIs","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/builders-library/making-retries-safe-with-idempotent-APIs/","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"Read on 2026-10-14; the page text does not clearly show a publication date","locator":"Official Builders’ Library page, read 2026-10-14; first two paragraphs of Retrying and side effects; caller-provided identifier passage under Reducing client complexity; Same client request ID, different intent.","claim_support":["The author explains how uncertain outcomes after timeouts may cause repeated side effects, introducing request identifiers and handling of identical IDs with different parameters."],"limits":["A technical-design explanation with hypothetical timeout scenarios, not a verified incident or this lesson's tool measurement. Specific APIs still require checking their own contracts."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"theory-v2","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v2, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v2 / 2026-11-16","locator":"13.11 AI Boundaries in Dynamic Operation; Chinese source lines 2888–2891; SHA-256 6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v2; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-13-11/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-13-11/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-13-11/source/S-13-11.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-13-11/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text and section locations correspond to v2; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"f57fa97c6d052cea510b5dadf90831ac0e7bb225d3f2c24ea78a123d38e2d475","source_sha256":"6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c"}},"13.11.1":{"title":"13.11.1 AI Material Checking and Tool-Action Chains","lead":"Finding a source still leaves questions about citation accuracy, whether the original passage supports the statement, and whether world facts have sufficient grounds. Tool actions likewise require separating “completed” in text from actual completed execution.","explanation":["Chinese source location: lines 2892–2901; original-site location /theory/part-13#section-13-11-1. See the theory-v2 source for the current full text; the historical research manuscript and excerpts remain downloadable in their original versions.","The four material checks have independent objects. First, does the source actually exist and can it be located? Second, are author, title, date, page numbers, and other citation details correct? Third, does the passage in context and under its limits support the current sentence? Fourth, does the reality described by that sentence have sufficient grounds? Passing one does not automatically carry over to the next: a real article can be miscited, and a faithful quotation can transmit source error. [T-check]","If a given source does not mention a fact, one can only say that this source does not support the statement, not that the statement must be false. An explicit denial backed by method is a different kind of evidence. Local knowledge, oral accounts, and protected material do not cease to exist because they lack a public webpage. Appropriate identity, timing, cross-checking, and controlled access can be designed without requiring all sensitive details to be published for traceability. [T-check]","Retrieval augmentation can help obtain material, and consistency or uncertainty detection may identify some errors, but effects depend on retrieval coverage, shared sources, and error types. This section explains limits of these functions; it supplies no improvement estimate for any system. Reproducible errors can also be highly consistent. Multiple models repeating the same inaccurate source do not constitute multiple independent empirical supports. [T-check]","A formal checker can confirm a derivation under explicit premises. For example, a=2 and b=3 imply a+b=5, but correct calculation does not prove that a real warehouse contains 2 and 3 items. The checker's conditional conclusion remains useful; responsibility for establishing its premises must stay with the corresponding factual verification. Formal correctness and empirical truth must neither be merged nor used to negate each other. [T-check]","The action chain separates recommendation, tool request, application execution, external state, and consequences. A model saying “stopped” is only generated text; check whether a specific stop request exists, whether the executor received it, whether in-flight actions terminated, and what happened to consequences. A deletion status in the interface does not prove downstream copies disappeared, and withdrawing a recommendation does not guarantee taking effect before an irreversible action. [T-check；T-end]","Actual protection and correction permissions must be exercisable in the relevant system. A reviewer with only a comment box and no effective stopping channel cannot treat comments as an execution brake. Even a brake button requires knowing which queues and stages it controls. Human involvement does not automatically mean edits propagated in time. This connects to §13.8's requirements for material, time, capability, and stopping resources. [T-check；T-human]","NIST2024 §2.2 explicitly discusses confabulation that can include logic or citations. This supports verifying explanatory prose too, rather than substituting explanation length for source support. It is itself a risk document, not proof that this lesson's four checks eliminate every error or that a factual audit has been completed. [P-NIST]","The strongest objection is that endless source checking would prevent any conclusion from being delivered. The four checks should target the factual importance and intended use of the task, stating current support and remaining gaps. A task requiring only accurate representation of a document can finish at that representation, but must not incidentally claim that every worldly fact in the document has been verified. A bounded endpoint can genuinely be completed; its conditions must be explicit. [T-check；T-end]"],"argumentSteps":["Locate the source and check citation identity and version.","Return to the original passage's context and check support scope and qualifications sentence by sentence.","Separately verify worldly facts according to their importance to the task; do not equate absence of mention with falsity.","Check source independence, coverage, and counterexamples; formal checking proves only derivations within explicit premises.","For actions, separately verify recommendations, requests, execution, external state, and consequences; stopping and correction must be practically exercisable."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Unmentioned by the source","leftText":"This source does not support the statement.","rightTitle":"The statement is false","rightText":"Requires sufficient contrary evidence; omission alone does not entail it."},{"leftTitle":"Agreement among multiple models","leftText":"Outputs are identical or similar.","rightTitle":"Multiple independent pieces of evidence","rightText":"Independence of sources and production paths requires separate checks."},{"leftTitle":"Correct formal derivation","leftText":"The conclusion holds under explicit premises.","rightTitle":"True premises","rightText":"Still require real-world evidence."},{"leftTitle":"Interface state","leftText":"What the current interface displays.","rightTitle":"Downstream state","rightText":"Whether dependent systems actually changed requires separate checks."}],"boundaries":["Source-text boundary: faithfully representing an erroneous source does not establish the facts.","Source-text boundary: oral/local/protected material does not cease to exist because it lacks a public webpage.","Teaching counterexample: two models citing the same incorrect table do not turn their matching answers into two independent supports."],"questions":["Is a document's real existence enough to trust an AI conclusion?","Can an oral account be treated as nonexistent if no public webpage is found?","If two models both say stopped, has execution stopped?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-13-11/figures/four-checks.png","caseLinks":[{"title":"Generated explanations and citations can also be inaccurate","href":"https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.600-1.pdf"}],"caseNotes":[{"title":"Generated explanations and citations can also be inaccurate","text":"NIST AI600-1 §2.2 includes inaccurate logic and citations in its discussion of confabulation risk.","sourceLabel":"NIST AI 600-1: generative-AI risk document","sourceUrl":"https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.600-1.pdf","sourceLocator":"2024 edition; PDF p. 5/printed p. 1, Introduction; PDF p. 10/printed p. 6, §2.2 Confabulation; PDF p. 22/printed p. 18, GOVERN 3.2.","boundary":"Supports only the types of risk requiring verification; gives neither a product's error rate nor the net effect of the four checks.","href":"https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.600-1.pdf"}],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"13.11.1","start_line":2972,"end_line":2979,"startLine":2972,"endLine":2979}],"outputId":"section-13-11-1","sourceHash":"51795bb7988f2669a324a52666c320cfba1382f597f7b5c6a1431844572da1fb","teachingExamples":[{"title":"Teaching construction: different outcomes of the four checks","text":"Constructed source document D exists, and the cited author and date are correct. Its original passage says, “3 of the 5 projects in this sample were delayed.” The AI writes, “60% of all projects were delayed.” Although the source and citation details are correct, the sample has been expanded into a population claim, so the third check, support, fails. “D reports delays in 3/5 of its sample” is a faithful representation, and 3÷5=60% is correct. But if the sample records themselves are wrong, the worldly facts remain unverified.","assumptions":"The document, projects, and numbers are all constructed to distinguish representation, arithmetic, and extrapolation.","boundary":"This is not an estimate of actual delay rates and does not prove any organization's dereliction."},{"title":"Teaching construction: timely edits and edits made too late","text":"A constructed automated sending system submits an irrevocable delivery at t=10; a reviewer edits the text at t=11. The edit was indeed saved, but that does not mean the message sent at t=10 was changed. Check the original request, execution time, target copy, and whether a separate correction arrived. Do not delete the historical failure to make the latest version seem correct throughout.","assumptions":"Constructed time units and system; no actual message was sent.","boundary":"Only illustrates temporal order and state differences; actual recall capability must be checked in the specific system."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Source-text boundary: faithfully representing an erroneous source does not establish the facts.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-check"]},{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Source-text boundary: oral/local/protected material does not cease to exist because it lacks a public webpage.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-check"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Teaching counterexample: two models citing the same incorrect table do not turn their matching answers into two independent supports.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-check"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"Is a document's real existence enough to trust an AI conclusion?","answer":"No. Citation details, original-passage support, and grounds for relevant worldly facts must also be checked.","basis":"Four checks."},{"question":"Can an oral account be treated as nonexistent if no public webpage is found?","answer":"No. Preserve appropriate verification and disclosure scope.","basis":"Knowledge-source boundary."},{"question":"If two models both say stopped, has execution stopped?","answer":"That cannot confirm it. Check the corresponding request, executor, in-flight status, and consequences.","basis":"Text and the execution chain."}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-13-11/figures/four-checks.png","alt":"Source existence, correct citation, passage support, and sufficient factual grounds are four distinct checks.","caption":"Passing an earlier check cannot automatically certify a later one."},{"path":"/reading-assets/S-13-11/figures/action-chain.png","alt":"Collect separate evidence for recommendation, request, application execution, external state, and task consequences.","caption":"The word “complete” must be tied to the actual layer and task."}],"diagramBrief":"Two static diagrams: four material checks and the five-layer action chain. Future interaction could mark evidence and unknowns layer by layer; one green receipt must not automatically illuminate downstream stages.","applicability":{"realCase":"applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"This unit concerns methodological judgment. Actual sources support only the listed facts; the diagram and teaching construction each retain their own status."},"openQuestions":["No blind testing of actual AI answers was conducted; the effects of retrieval, consistency checks, and formal checking require separate task data."],"taskId":"S-13-11","sources":[{"id":"T-main","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§13.11, Chinese source lines 2832–2835","claim_support":["AI dynamic work, prohibitions on overreach, and variable-candidate responsibilities."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"T-check","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§13.11.1, Chinese source lines 2836–2845","claim_support":["Four material checks, boundaries of retrieval and consistency, formal checking, and tool-action chains."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"T-end","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§13.11.2, Chinese source lines 2846–2863","claim_support":["Five endpoint levels, concurrency/timeouts, stopping, and failure preservation."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"T-dynamic","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§13.10, Chinese source lines 2814–2831","claim_support":["Dynamic candidates, conditional branches, and normative permissions."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"T-human","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§13.8, Chinese source lines 2794–2801","claim_support":["Resources and stopping permissions for effective human review."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"P-NIST","title":"NIST AI 600-1: generative-AI risk document","url":"https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.600-1.pdf","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"2024","locator":"2024 edition; PDF p. 5/printed p. 1, Introduction; PDF p. 10/printed p. 6, §2.2 Confabulation; PDF p. 22/printed p. 18, GOVERN 3.2.","claim_support":["This edition's voluntary companion document discusses confabulation, erroneous logic/citation risks, division of roles, and recommendations for feedback and redress."],"limits":["A governance text and risk discussion, not this lesson's experiment, a quantitative estimate of all models' performance, or current mandatory law."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"P-AWS","title":"Malcolm Featonby：Making retries safe with idempotent APIs","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/builders-library/making-retries-safe-with-idempotent-APIs/","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"Read on 2026-10-14; the page text does not clearly show a publication date","locator":"Official Builders’ Library page, read 2026-10-14; first two paragraphs of Retrying and side effects; caller-provided identifier passage under Reducing client complexity; Same client request ID, different intent.","claim_support":["The author explains how uncertain outcomes after timeouts may cause repeated side effects, introducing request identifiers and handling of identical IDs with different parameters."],"limits":["A technical-design explanation with hypothetical timeout scenarios, not a verified incident or this lesson's tool measurement. Specific APIs still require checking their own contracts."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"theory-v2","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v2, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v2 / 2026-11-16","locator":"13.11.1 AI material checks and tool action chains; Chinese source lines 2892–2901; SHA-256 6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v2; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-13-11/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-13-11/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-13-11/source/S-13-11.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-13-11/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text and section locations correspond to v2; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"6a544b2af5cb389c667bf253fbaa146d205bbe846a037403dfd26b8915b2760a","source_sha256":"6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c"}},"13.11.2":{"title":"13.11.2 Task endpoints and verifiable execution chains","lead":"Completion must match the endpoint the user requested: delivering a draft, changing an external record, and actually delivering a remedy are different tasks. Verifying the required endpoint accurately avoids treating receipts as outcomes or delaying narrow tasks indefinitely.","explanation":["Source location: Chinese source lines 2902–2919; original site location /theory/part-13#section-13-11-2. See the theory-v2 source for the current full text; the historical research original and excerpts remain available for download and checking in their original versions.","The task endpoint is determined by the user's request or the institutional task. If only a draft is requested, saving it correctly, making it openable, and satisfying content requirements can complete delivery. Changing an external record also requires checking the target object's state. Delivering a remedy further requires checking that resources, rights, or services arrive. Consequence requirements for a broad task cannot negate completion of a narrow task, and success on a narrow task cannot replace an unfinished broad task. [T-end]","The recommendation-formation layer checks the target, material, object to be changed, and permitted scope, preventing generation from being mistaken for adoption. The tool-request layer checks action, parameters, current version, and permissions, preventing a correct recommendation from being sent to the wrong object. The application-execution layer checks receipt, execution or refusal, and duplicate-request semantics; delivery and queueing are not yet execution success. [T-end]","The external-state layer checks whether the target and dependent objects changed as expected, avoiding a local label update while actual execution still reads old state. The task-consequence layer checks the specified benefit/protection endpoint and residuals: correcting a record may be complete, but a request to remedy a lost opportunity also requires checking whether the opportunity, resources, or service actually recovered. Not every correction fully reverses consequences; residuals must be retained honestly. [T-end]","For concurrency, first distinguish independent reads, independent modifications, and shared writes to the same object. Shared-state modifications require ordering, version preconditions, or conflict handling. Two individually permitted actions do not justify ignoring lost updates from simultaneous overwrites. Before irreversible action, recheck that permissions and stopping conditions remain valid; initial permission does not necessarily cover subsequent changes in object, use, or deadline. [T-end]","A request timeout only means no definite result arrived within the waiting window. Execution may not have happened, may be underway, or may have finished with its receipt lost. Do not assume non-execution and repeat the action. First check by request identifier or object state, then decide whether to retry. Where a system provides an idempotency contract, handle the same request under that actual contract; do not mistake a new identifier for a retry of the old request. All applications need not share one log format. [T-end；P-AWS]","The AWS technical article explains uncertainty after a timeout, introduces client request identifiers for distinguishing duplicate requests, and discusses rejecting different parameters under the same identifier. It publicly describes concrete technical semantics. Its timeout scenario is hypothetical, not an actual incident. This lesson did not operate any interface or verify production performance. [P-AWS]","Stopping has at least four separate items: issuing the stop, executor confirmation, termination of in-flight actions, and remedying consequences already produced. If the first two pass, report only “stop sent and receipt confirmed,” not that all tasks and side effects have disappeared. This does not deny the first two steps' progress; it preserves the endpoints still requiring verification. [T-end]","Stopping the main task does not mean removing all subsequent protections. If safe withdrawal still requires bounded actions, continue under their independent responsibilities, permissions, and actual conditions—for example, checking in-flight status or preserving necessary evidence. Do not restart the stopped main task under the pretext of withdrawal. A repair failure must retain the failed object and real-world consequences; new normal outputs must not overwrite them. [T-end]","The strongest objection is that layered verification might pursue consequences throughout a person's life without end. The source explicitly bounds verification by the requested endpoint: verify the draft for draft delivery, the target and necessary dependencies for a record change, and the specified arrival only for remedy delivery. A good completion report states completed layers, unknowns, and residuals item by item, neither overpromising nor hiding what has actually been completed. [T-end]"],"argumentSteps":["First state the task endpoint and acceptance object, distinguishing content delivery, record changes, and remedy arrival.","Use the five-layer chain to check target parameters, versions, permissions, receipt and execution, target and dependent states, and required consequences.","Distinguish independent reads/independent modifications/shared writes; preserve order or check version conflicts for shared writes.","Record timeout as unknown; verify by request identifier/object before deciding on retry, without blindly repeating actions.","Check stopping item by item: sending, confirmation, in-flight termination, and consequence remedies. Bounded safe withdrawal needs independent grounds.","Report reached endpoints and residuals; retain failures and new versions without overwriting history."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Draft delivery complete","leftText":"Saved, openable, and compliant with content requirements.","rightTitle":"Policy implemented","rightText":"Requires evidence of external adoption and execution."},{"leftTitle":"Request timeout","leftText":"The result remains unclear.","rightTitle":"Not executed","rightText":"Only one possibility; it cannot be inferred directly."},{"leftTitle":"Stop received","leftText":"The executor confirms receipt.","rightTitle":"Full termination/remedy","rightText":"In-flight status and existing consequences require separate checks."},{"leftTitle":"Normal output after repair","leftText":"The new version's current state may be correct.","rightTitle":"The old failure no longer exists","rightText":"Historical objects and residual consequences cannot be deleted."}],"boundaries":["Source-text boundary: a narrow endpoint can truly be complete without waiting for every life consequence; a broad endpoint cannot be replaced by a frontend receipt.","Source-text boundary: timeout is not non-execution; shared writes require versions/ordering/conflict handling.","Source-text boundary: stopping and remedy are separate; new normal outputs must not overwrite repair failures."],"questions":["If only a draft is requested, must adoption first be proved before completion?","After a timeout, may the request immediately be repeated with a new ID?","If someone clicked stop but in-flight actions remain unknown, how should this be reported?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-13-11/figures/endpoints.png","caseLinks":[{"title":"AWS's technical explanation of request identity on retries","href":"https://aws.amazon.com/builders-library/making-retries-safe-with-idempotent-APIs/"}],"caseNotes":[{"title":"AWS's technical explanation of request identity on retries","text":"An AWS Builders’ Library article describes uncertain outcomes after timeouts and request-identifier design for controlling duplicate side effects; the same identifier with different parameters must be distinguished.","sourceLabel":"Malcolm Featonby：Making retries safe with idempotent APIs","sourceUrl":"https://aws.amazon.com/builders-library/making-retries-safe-with-idempotent-APIs/","sourceLocator":"Official Builders’ Library page, read 2026-10-14; first two paragraphs of Retrying and side effects; caller-provided identifier passage under Reducing client complexity; Same client request ID, different intent.","boundary":"This is a design explanation in technical documentation, and its hypothetical scenario is not an actual incident. A specific interface's behavior requires checking its contract separately; no actual invocation was made here.","href":"https://aws.amazon.com/builders-library/making-retries-safe-with-idempotent-APIs/"}],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"13.11.2","start_line":2980,"end_line":3001,"startLine":2980,"endLine":3001}],"outputId":"section-13-11-2","sourceHash":"51795bb7988f2669a324a52666c320cfba1382f597f7b5c6a1431844572da1fb","teachingExamples":[{"title":"Teaching construction: lost updates and version verification","text":"A constructed shared document v1 contains value 10. A reads v1 intending to add 2; B also reads v1 intending to add 3. If each overwrites the whole value with 12 and 13, the final value may be only 13, losing A's update. If the task requires preserving both increments, the correct total is 10+2+3=15. One approach rejects B's stale write on a version mismatch, rereads v2, and merges under the still-valid task.","assumptions":"Constructed integers and whole-value overwrite semantics. Not every concurrent write loses updates, and no system was actually configured.","boundary":"15 holds only if both increments should be retained and authorization remains valid; rechecking cannot let the algorithm expand its own permissions."},{"title":"Teaching construction: genuine completion at different endpoints","text":"Constructed user A requests only a scheduling draft. The saved file opens and meets content requirements, so draft completion can be reported, but not notification of employees. User B wants a system record corrected; besides a success receipt, the target and relevant caches must be read back. User C requests a missed service be made up; even after the record changes, service arrival and residual harm still require checking. The three cannot substitute for one another.","assumptions":"Constructed requests, files, and services; no actual user data or real-world actions.","boundary":"This does not require every draft to be executed; the actual request determines the task endpoint."},{"title":"Teaching construction: reporting stopping by layer","text":"A constructed queue sends a stop at t=1; the executor confirms at t=2; at t=3, tasks already taken from the queue remain in flight; termination is verified only at t=5. At t=2, report only that the request was confirmed. At t=5, report in-flight termination within the checked scope. If erroneous records were already publicly displayed before t=1, they still require correction on independent grounds and verification of arrival. Stopping the main queue does not establish that all consequences disappeared.","assumptions":"Constructed times and queue; no actual shutdown, deletion, or external sending occurred.","boundary":"Completion can be declared only within the verified scope; separately list grounds for safe withdrawal and residual issues."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Source-text boundary: a narrow endpoint can truly be complete without waiting for every life consequence; a broad endpoint cannot be replaced by a frontend receipt.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-end"]},{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Source-text boundary: timeout is not non-execution; shared writes require versions/ordering/conflict handling.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-end"]},{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Source-text boundary: stopping and remedy are separate; new normal outputs must not overwrite repair failures.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-end"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"If only a draft is requested, must adoption first be proved before completion?","answer":"No. Accept the draft based on saving, openability, and content requirements; do not claim adoption.","basis":"Narrow endpoint."},{"question":"After a timeout, may the request immediately be repeated with a new ID?","answer":"A timeout cannot establish non-execution. First check request or object state, then decide under the contract and permissions.","basis":"Avoid duplicate side effects."},{"question":"If someone clicked stop but in-flight actions remain unknown, how should this be reported?","answer":"Accurately report stop sent/confirmed, with in-flight termination and consequence remedies still awaiting verification.","basis":"Four stopping items."}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-13-11/figures/endpoints.png","alt":"Drafts, record changes, and actual remedies have different acceptance objects.","caption":"The endpoint comes from the request; neither expand it without limit nor quietly narrow it."},{"path":"/reading-assets/S-13-11/figures/execution.png","alt":"Check versions for shared writes, check state before retrying timeouts, and confirm the four stopping items separately while retaining failures.","caption":"Constructed example 10+2+3=15; not an executed production system."}],"diagramBrief":"Two static diagrams show three task endpoints and execution checks for concurrency, timeouts, and stopping. Future interaction could mark each layer checked/unknown, without substituting a button response for actual completion.","applicability":{"realCase":"applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"This unit concerns methodological judgment. Actual sources support only the listed facts; the diagram and teaching construction each retain their own status."},"openQuestions":["No actual external modification, stopping, or remedy was executed, and no specific system's idempotency, cache propagation, or recall capability was tested."],"taskId":"S-13-11","sources":[{"id":"T-main","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§13.11, Chinese source lines 2832–2835","claim_support":["AI dynamic work, prohibitions on overreach, and variable-candidate responsibilities."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"T-check","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§13.11.1, Chinese source lines 2836–2845","claim_support":["Four material checks, boundaries of retrieval and consistency, formal checking, and tool-action chains."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"T-end","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§13.11.2, Chinese source lines 2846–2863","claim_support":["Five endpoint levels, concurrency/timeouts, stopping, and failure preservation."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"T-dynamic","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§13.10, Chinese source lines 2814–2831","claim_support":["Dynamic candidates, conditional branches, and normative permissions."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"T-human","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§13.8, Chinese source lines 2794–2801","claim_support":["Resources and stopping permissions for effective human review."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"P-NIST","title":"NIST AI 600-1: generative-AI risk document","url":"https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.600-1.pdf","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"2024","locator":"2024 edition; PDF p. 5/printed p. 1, Introduction; PDF p. 10/printed p. 6, §2.2 Confabulation; PDF p. 22/printed p. 18, GOVERN 3.2.","claim_support":["This edition's voluntary companion document discusses confabulation, erroneous logic/citation risks, division of roles, and recommendations for feedback and redress."],"limits":["A governance text and risk discussion, not this lesson's experiment, a quantitative estimate of all models' performance, or current mandatory law."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"P-AWS","title":"Malcolm Featonby：Making retries safe with idempotent APIs","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/builders-library/making-retries-safe-with-idempotent-APIs/","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"Read on 2026-10-14; the page text does not clearly show a publication date","locator":"Official Builders’ Library page, read 2026-10-14; first two paragraphs of Retrying and side effects; caller-provided identifier passage under Reducing client complexity; Same client request ID, different intent.","claim_support":["The author explains how uncertain outcomes after timeouts may cause repeated side effects, introducing request identifiers and handling of identical IDs with different parameters."],"limits":["A technical-design explanation with hypothetical timeout scenarios, not a verified incident or this lesson's tool measurement. Specific APIs still require checking their own contracts."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"theory-v2","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v2, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v2 / 2026-11-16","locator":"13.11.2 Task endpoints and verifiable execution chains; Chinese source lines 2902–2919; SHA-256 6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v2; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-13-11/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-13-11/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-13-11/source/S-13-11.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-13-11/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text and section locations correspond to v2; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"a4f5796a77162f8bfc896e3d6651dc97993b2f7a7870698e27d6a102596e5574","source_sha256":"6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c"}}}},"headers":[],"relativePath":"en/theory/part-13.md","filePath":"en/theory/part-13.md"}`),c={name:"en/theory/part-13.md"};function d(l,e,u,p,h,m){const i=s("SectionLesson");return r(),o("div",null,[e[0]||(e[0]=t('<div class="theory-source"><p><a id="part_13"></a></p><h1 id="part-title-13" tabindex="-1">Part 13 Interfaces and Tools <a class="header-anchor" href="#part-title-13" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Part 13 Interfaces and Tools {#part-title-13}&quot;">​</a></h1><p>This Part converts the objects, propositions, scales, mechanisms, actors, multi-circle joint states, and dynamic projection requirements defined in the preceding 12 Parts into limited judgments that can be observed, questioned, reviewed, and withdrawn. It adds no facts about the world. Completing a form does not establish a fact. A D0 candidate object record and a nine-axis scale record must exist before the tool workflow begins. If the object, boundary, scale, time window, identity criterion, or intended use changes, existing judgments must not simply be carried over.</p><p>The tool workflow has two levels. The seven gates are the only main workflow. The five gates and 13 steps elaborate the diagnosis<sup class="footnote-ref"><a href="#fn1" id="fnref1">[1]</a></sup>. The seven gates determine whether a judgment can proceed to the next level. In complex cases the five gates and 13 steps spell out the material that must be submitted. They do not issue a separate “pass” that competes with the seven gates or produce a second set of workflow statuses.</p><p>The tool layer confers no authorization. Even when the workflow, evidence, and strong-judgment kit are all complete, the outputs are limited to descriptions, candidate mechanisms, comparative judgments, strong-judgment candidates, or requirements for action. Real-world action still requires a separate structured selection record, the 10-component C12 bridge, one and the same valid atomic J authorization, and O1–O4. A tool output cannot substitute for any of them.</p><p>The following workflow governs structured judgments formed under this framework and their handoff. Reading, checking against the original text, and clarifying a low-impact question can use concise records suited to the task. Skipping the full tool workflow is no basis for declaring that established facts in the domain do not exist. Formal diagnosis, foresight, or high-impact use requires the corresponding full contract<sup class="footnote-ref"><a href="#fn2" id="fnref2">[2]</a></sup>. Renaming an output cannot reduce the responsibilities it carries.</p></div>',1)),n(i,{section:"intro-13-part"}),e[1]||(e[1]=t('<div class="theory-source"><h2 id="section-13-1" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">13.1</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Four Conversions <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-13-1" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;13.1 Four Conversions {#section-13-1}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Each conversion adds a responsibility for recording. Replacing terms from the theory with labels for real-world situations does not accomplish this. If any step fails, the outputs that depend on it stop before that step. A later step’s completed fields cannot make up for the failure. Other independent judgments are reviewed separately on their own grounds and intended uses.</p><div class="xk-table"><table><thead><tr><th>ID</th><th>Conversion</th><th>Required Result</th><th>Where to Stop If It Fails</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>IT1</td><td>Structural variables to observable signals</td><td>A record of signals that specifies the object, scale, observation position, time window, measurement protocol, and missing-data status</td><td>A conceptual mapping or a gap in the material</td></tr><tr><td>IT2</td><td>Observable signals to strength of evidence</td><td>An evidence record that separates sources, observations, comparison conditions, coverage, counterexamples, alternative explanations, and uncertainty</td><td>Ungraded material or a hypothesis to test</td></tr><tr><td>IT3</td><td>Strength of evidence to level of judgment</td><td>A judgment with a proposition contract, limits of applicability, contrary conditions, falsification conditions, and withdrawal conditions</td><td>A candidate explanation or an observational assessment</td></tr><tr><td>IT4</td><td>Level of judgment to requirements and ceiling for action</td><td>A list of affected positions, the protection floor, prohibited actions, stopping, appeal, rollback, and outstanding N/J/O/C12 requirements</td><td>Description, a need for more evidence, or a requirement to take no action</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>IT1 prevents a narrative about structure from being treated as an observed fact. IT2 prevents citations, materials, or declarations of position from being treated directly as evidence. IT3 prevents correlation, analogy, or one narrative of how a mechanism works from being elevated to a conclusion. IT4 prevents a leap from a factual judgment to action. Passing all four conversions leaves authorization status unchanged.</p><p>If any IT conversion fails, dependent later conversions may only be marked <code>not_run</code>. The propositions, comparative judgments, strong-judgment candidates, and requirements for action that they support must be stopped or downgraded. Downstream conclusions that share the same gap must not regain their ceiling through renaming, splitting, or opening another branch. A later <code>not_run</code> only preserves the chain of failure. Completing the kit cannot repair the break. A judgment may retain its original standing if it does not depend on the failed step, has independent grounds, and its use meets its own requirements. If the gap affects the overall source status, a shared factual premise, the object boundary, or lawful conditions for use of the entire task, stop or downgrade all dependent outputs together. If a gate required for high-impact adoption fails, calling the same judgment an ordinary opinion does not allow it to retain its original use.</p></div>',1)),n(i,{section:"13.1"}),e[2]||(e[2]=t('<div class="theory-source"><h2 id="section-13-2" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">13.2</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Using the Universal Primitives and Human Variable Interfaces (11+11) <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-13-2" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;13.2 Using the Universal Primitives and Human Variable Interfaces (11+11) {#section-13-2}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>HV01–HV11 in Part 7 are the 11 interfaces for the human structured world. U01–U11 in Part 3 are the universal structural primitives. The tool layer can use both sets in the same case. Matching numbers, equal counts, or similar questions do not establish one-to-one equivalence.</p><p>At minimum, record the universal or human interface ID, effective object, nine-axis scale, selected support route, instance evidence, alternative explanations, missing-data status, judgment ceiling, and action ceiling. H1/H4/H5 may enter the specified routes only through the corresponding H-instance in each case. G1–G4 can support empirical claims only through a G-instance frozen before results. An interface card can raise a question. Completing its fields cannot establish facts about the object.</p></div>',1)),n(i,{section:"13.2"}),e[3]||(e[3]=t('<div class="theory-source"><h2 id="section-13-3" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">13.3</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>The Seven Gates as the Only Main Workflow <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-13-3" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;13.3 The Seven Gates as the Only Main Workflow {#section-13-3}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Run the seven gates for each specific proposition in dependency order. After one gate fails, dependent later gates may only be marked <code>not_run</code>, and the corresponding judgments and requirements for action stop or are downgraded. A later <code>not_run</code> cannot raise the ceiling lowered by the failed gate. Acknowledging that the object or evidence failed a gate while allowing dependent later procedures to report their own pass is also prohibited. Judgments that do not depend on the failed step must retain independent evidence and undergo review of their intended use. Another proposition passing cannot conceal this proposition’s failure. If overall source status, a shared factual premise, the object boundary, or lawful conditions for use of the task cease to hold, all dependent outputs stop together. When the object, evidence version, affected positions, or intended use changes substantially, rerun the workflow from the earliest gate affected.</p><div class="xk-table"><table><thead><tr><th>ID</th><th>Gate</th><th>Core Question</th><th>Output Ceiling If It Does Not Pass</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>IG1</td><td>Effective Object Gate</td><td>Do the object, boundary, nine-axis scale, time window, and K support the current judgment?</td><td>Redefine the object, leave the scale unknown, or review object conversion</td></tr><tr><td>IG2</td><td>Evidence Traceability Gate</td><td>Are sources, materials, observations, evidence, counterexamples, cases, and judgments distinct and traceable?</td><td>Organization of materials, candidate questions, or hypotheses to test</td></tr><tr><td>IG3</td><td>Affected Positions Gate</td><td>Which positions will see their circumstances change through direct, indirect, second-order, cross-domain, or intertemporal effects?</td><td>Supplement the accounting of impacts. Suspend strong judgments and high-impact uses</td></tr><tr><td>IG4</td><td>Power and Anti-Retaliation Gate</td><td>Can people in low-power positions safely add evidence, rebut claims, refuse, stop, and appeal?</td><td>Draw no adverse inference from silence. Lower the levels of publication and judgment</td></tr><tr><td>IG5</td><td>Mediation and Reflexivity Gate</td><td>Do metrics, platforms, AI, public evaluation, or the act of diagnosis change the evidence and the object?</td><td>High reflexivity remains unresolved. Restate the judgment as a conditional state transition</td></tr><tr><td>IG6</td><td>Strong-Judgment Procedure Gate</td><td>Have all SJ1–SJ8 items been submitted for the proposition whose intended use and foreseeable impacts trigger the strong-judgment procedure? Public dissemination requires closer scrutiny. It does not by itself elevate all analyses to strong judgments</td><td>Stop the relevant strong judgments and high-impact uses. Review separately any comparative judgment that stands independently and meets the requirements for its use</td></tr><tr><td>IG7</td><td>Action Ceiling Gate</td><td>What is the most that may be required? Which actions are prohibited, and when are stopping, appeal, and rollback called for?</td><td>Description, gaps in evidence, no action, or continued deliberation</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Passing all seven gates indicates only that the applicable recording requirements have no gaps. It does not establish that the conclusion is true. Empirical propositions are still judged under their proposition contracts and instance evidence. Normative objectives still require explicit N premises. Real-world action still requires choice, C12, J, and O procedures. In particular, IG7 can only record whether the external normative and authorization layers meet their requirements. The tool workflow cannot mark an action as “authorized” on its own.</p><p>IG6 has a specific applicability rule. An ordinary L1/L2 output may mark it <code>not_applicable</code>, but the record must state why the strong-judgment procedure is not triggered in this case. This does not block IG7. Once IG6 is marked <code>passed</code>, the same run record must link a complete SJ1–SJ8, with every item marked <code>passed</code>. A “checked” string or an empty rationale cannot substitute for the eight results.</p><p>The scope of identification for IG3 is determined under §14.4.1. Positions that section requires to be listed in full must be listed in full; for other positions, the record must state how far they were traced and why tracing stopped. For explanation and diagnosis tasks without a comparison of options, the test is whether further tracing could change the conclusion or its grade, the scope of use, or protective arrangements.</p></div>',1)),n(i,{section:"13.3"}),e[4]||(e[4]=t('<div class="theory-source"><h2 id="section-13-4" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">13.4</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>The Five Gates and 13 Steps for Elaborating a Diagnosis <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-13-4" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;13.4 The Five Gates and 13 Steps for Elaborating a Diagnosis {#section-13-4}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Run the five gates and 13 steps when the object is complex, responsibility is high, reflexivity is high, or the seven gates call for more detailed evidence. The five gates are local checks within this elaboration. They do not generate a second set of main workflow statuses.</p><div class="xk-table"><table><thead><tr><th>ID</th><th>Elaboration Gate</th><th>Material That Must Be Developed</th><th>Corresponding Main Gates</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>FG1</td><td>Object Gate</td><td>Object, boundary, scale, and time window</td><td>IG1</td></tr><tr><td>FG2</td><td>Evidence Gate</td><td>Cost of signals, sources, weights, and review</td><td>IG2</td></tr><tr><td>FG3</td><td>Scale Gate</td><td>Nine-axis scale, objects and evidence at the scale, and changes in scale</td><td>IG1, IG2</td></tr><tr><td>FG4</td><td>Responsibility Gate</td><td>Effects on rights/resources, contrary conditions, repair windows, appeals, and review</td><td>IG3, IG4, IG6</td></tr><tr><td>FG5</td><td>Observation Gate</td><td>Baseline before observation, reactions during observation, reactions after publication, and a ceiling on recursion</td><td>IG5</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The 13 steps form an auditable sequence from the real-world question to write-back.</p><div class="xk-table"><table><thead><tr><th>ID</th><th>Step</th><th>Minimum Deliverable</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>DS01</td><td>Start With the Question</td><td>A real-world question in language outside the framework, the requester, and the intended use</td></tr><tr><td>DS02</td><td>Identify the Object and Boundary</td><td>Effective object, subsystems, higher-level environment, and external channels</td></tr><tr><td>DS03</td><td>Separate Signal Types</td><td>Separate columns for facts, behavior, resources, feedback, narratives, feelings, conjecture, and noise</td></tr><tr><td>DS04</td><td>Record Participation in Observation</td><td>Awareness of being observed, observer power, impacts of publication, and candidate responses</td></tr><tr><td>DS05</td><td>Identify the Dominant Constraint</td><td>Candidate constraints, the selected constraint, evidence for selection, and competing constraints</td></tr><tr><td>DS06</td><td>Record the State and Window</td><td>References to S0–S6 prototypes for applicable objects, positions within the state, parallel subsystems, and contrary conditions. If inapplicable, state why and retain the domain’s own states and windows</td></tr><tr><td>DS07</td><td>Examine the Core Structure</td><td>Anchor, uptake layer, transmission chain, boundary, and feedback write-back</td></tr><tr><td>DS08</td><td>Identify Key Variables for Protection</td><td>Variables to protect, paths to harm, resources, redundancy, and paths for repair/return flows</td></tr><tr><td>DS09</td><td>Record Uptake and Repayment</td><td>Cost bearers, recipients of return flows, rights to stop, long-term overextension, and shifting of harms</td></tr><tr><td>DS10</td><td>Identify Hidden Risks and Boundary Types</td><td>Maintenance debt, mismatches between fast and slow processes, metric substitution, distorted summaries, and candidate object conversions</td></tr><tr><td>DS11</td><td>Propose Candidate Mechanisms</td><td>Competing mechanisms that the material permits and that could change the judgment, the evidence each requires, evidence that weakens them, and rules for eliminating them. If no plausible competing explanation exists, state the scope of the search and the failure conditions. Do not invent mechanisms to meet a quota</td></tr><tr><td>DS12</td><td>Anticipate Branches</td><td>Triggers, early signals, contrary conditions, repair windows, and minimum requirements for action</td></tr><tr><td>DS13</td><td>Produce the Output and Write Back</td><td>Output in ordinary language, triggers for revision, scope of the record, review, withdrawal, and rollback</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>When DS06 uses S0–S6, it must do so only for human intentional collectives to which the prototypes apply. The prototypes must not be forced onto other objects. S0–S6 must not be treated as levels of maturity or as fate. Identifying an important variable for protection in DS08 can generate only a requirement for protection. Identifying uptake or overextension in DS09 cannot generate a duty to make sacrifices or establish full responsibility. Candidate mechanisms in DS11 must not bypass the causal contract. Branches in DS12 do not describe a certain future. If DS13 lacks review and withdrawal procedures, the judgment must not be entered into organizational records, eligibility, allocation, or public memory.</p></div>',1)),n(i,{section:"13.4"}),e[5]||(e[5]=t('<div class="theory-source"><h2 id="section-13-5" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">13.5</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>The Strong-Judgment Kit <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-13-5" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;13.5 The Strong-Judgment Kit {#section-13-5}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Strong judgments are defined by their uses and foreseeable impacts. Tone, length, and confidence of expression do not determine the category. A judgment enters the strong-judgment procedure if it is intended or reasonably foreseeable for use in real-world action or decisions about eligibility, rights, or resource allocation. The same applies if it has a material impact on a specific subject’s reputation and place in public memory, or if it is embedded in automated, organizational, or institutional decisions. Check the actual use of intermediate scores, recommendations, or material screening when they affect these decisions downstream. Public dissemination calls for greater scrutiny. Assess it in light of the object evaluated, factual allegations, identifiability, reach, and consequences. Publication alone is insufficient to raise every analytical output to a strong judgment. Ordinary work can reach definite conclusions when evaluating arguments, explaining mechanisms, or articulating normative views, provided the uses and impacts above are absent and the evidence and review meet the task’s requirements. Calling an output commentary, research, or a draft does not exempt it if it actually meets the strong-judgment conditions. A strong-judgment candidate may be registered only at L3, with all seven gates passed and each of the following eight items passed individually. It still confers no authorization to act in reality.</p><div class="xk-table"><table><thead><tr><th>ID</th><th>Component</th><th>Required Question</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>SJ1</td><td>Contrary conditions</td><td>What circumstances would weaken, reverse, or lead to withdrawal of the judgment?</td></tr><tr><td>SJ2</td><td>Repair window</td><td>Where is the window, what supports it, and when does it cease to hold?</td></tr><tr><td>SJ3</td><td>Evidence requirements</td><td>What evidence supports the judgment, what evidence is missing, and what evidence weighs against it?</td></tr><tr><td>SJ4</td><td>Access to appeal</td><td>Who can file an appeal, who is responsible, what is the time limit, and can the outcome change?</td></tr><tr><td>SJ5</td><td>Anti-retaliation protection</td><td>Which activities are protected, how is protection monitored, and how can retaliation be reported independently and remedied?</td></tr><tr><td>SJ6</td><td>Right to add evidence</td><td>How can evidence be supplemented, versions corrected, and downstream records synchronized?</td></tr><tr><td>SJ7</td><td>Trigger for external review</td><td>When is review triggered, who reviews independently, and can they access the materials and stop the result from taking effect?</td></tr><tr><td>SJ8</td><td>Rollback and write-back</td><td>Which state is restored, who carries out the repair, which version is authoritative, and how are updates synchronized?</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>If any item is missing, IG6 fails for that strong judgment. The judgment must not enter the category of strong-judgment candidate or retain its original high-impact use. It may be recorded only as an internal hypothesis, an open assertion, or a candidate mechanism within the limits of its materials and actual use. An L2 comparative judgment may remain if it does not depend on the missing item, has independent grounds, and does not trigger a strong-judgment use. Failure of a common premise still blocks all dependent judgments. Renaming or splitting must not be used to evade that limit. Access to appeal does not count as an effective appeal if it cannot change a record or action. Being external in name does not establish independence. A statement, apology, or release of a new version does not amount to actual rollback and repair.</p></div>',1)),n(i,{section:"13.5"}),e[6]||(e[6]=t('<div class="theory-source"><h2 id="section-13-6" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">13.6</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Diagnostic Levels <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-13-6" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;13.6 Diagnostic Levels {#section-13-6}&quot;">​</a></h2><div class="xk-table"><table><thead><tr><th>ID</th><th>Level</th><th>When It Applies</th><th>Output Ceiling</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>L1</td><td>Lightweight description and exploratory questions</td><td>Low complexity, low impact, reversibility, and limited information</td><td>Description, questions, and the next observation</td></tr><tr><td>L2</td><td>Standard reviewable judgment</td><td>A structural comparison is needed, but the judgment will not be used for action carrying high responsibility</td><td>A comparative judgment within stated limits or a candidate mechanism</td></tr><tr><td>L3</td><td>Strong-judgment candidate carrying high responsibility</td><td>Judgments with substantively high impact or embedded in automated, organizational, or institutional decisions. Public dissemination is reviewed in light of the object, factual allegations, identifiability, and consequences</td><td>A strong-judgment candidate after the seven gates and the kit pass</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>A higher level adds responsibility for recording and does not make a judgment “closer to the truth.” The level must be downgraded if evidence coverage declines, the object undergoes conversion, reflexivity becomes uncontrolled, or appeals or anti-retaliation protection fail. Urgency does not permit omitting the object, protection, or stopping conditions. When waiting is costly, the tool may only propose the narrowest, withdrawable, monitorable requirement for action, separately from the applicable authorization.</p></div>',1)),n(i,{section:"13.6"}),e[7]||(e[7]=t('<div class="theory-source"><h2 id="section-13-7" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">13.7</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Six Formal Tools <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-13-7" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;13.7 Six Formal Tools {#section-13-7}&quot;">​</a></h2><div class="xk-table"><table><thead><tr><th>ID</th><th>Tool</th><th>Purpose and Limits</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>TOOL-EVIDENCE-LEDGER</code></td><td>Source–Evidence Ledger</td><td>Keep sources, materials, observations, evidence, counterexamples, coverage, and uncertainty separate. It does not prove a proposition</td></tr><tr><td><code>TOOL-OPEN-ASSERTION</code></td><td>Open Assertion Record</td><td>Retain a provisional judgment that can be questioned, falsified, or withdrawn. Do not use it for action carrying high responsibility</td></tr><tr><td><code>TOOL-CLAIM-VALIDATION</code></td><td>Proposition Validation Form</td><td>Register proposition type, evidence, counterevidence, falsification conditions, impacts, and ceilings separately</td></tr><tr><td><code>TOOL-FORECAST-REGISTRY</code></td><td>Forecast Registration</td><td>Freeze the prediction, window, triggers, early signals, contrary signals, and targets for revision</td></tr><tr><td><code>TOOL-STRESS-TEST</code></td><td>Counterexample and Scale Stress Test</td><td>Use boundary cases, adversarial explanations, and changes in scale to locate failures and narrow the scope</td></tr><tr><td><code>TOOL-AI-BOUNDARY</code></td><td>AI Output Boundaries</td><td>Require disclosure of sources, identification of missing material and the responsible human, and recording of uncertainty, prohibited uses, review, and withdrawal</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The evidence ledger must not turn “has a source” into “is supported.” An open assertion cannot substitute for proposition validation. A failed prediction must be written back to the relevant proposition. An internal stress test is only a wind tunnel and does not supply external empirical support. Tools may cite one another’s record IDs, but must not validate one another circularly.</p><p>Automatic validation is suited to checking closed fields, whether references resolve, consistency of versions and statuses, and whether calculations can be reproduced. It cannot replace assessment of materials, testimony from people affected, causal identification, or choices about values. Programs should do the work that can be done mechanically so that readers can examine the actual grounds for judgments. Complete logs and clear prose can be kept separately with references between them.</p></div>',1)),n(i,{section:"13.7"}),e[8]||(e[8]=t('<div class="theory-source"><h2 id="section-13-8" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">13.8</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Limits on the Use of AI <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-13-8" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;13.8 Limits on the Use of AI {#section-13-8}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>AI can organize materials, check fields, compare versions, generate candidate questions, list alternative explanations, and find inconsistencies in formatting. Where the evidence and normative premises allow, it should also compare mechanisms, form refutable judgments, recommend options, and draft complete institutional arrangements, plans, and commentary. AI must not invent sources, treat information recalled from training as current evidence, express consent on someone’s behalf, interpret silence as consent, take the place of a deciding subject or authorizing authority, or hide uncertainty. It must not make high-risk real-world decisions independently.</p><p>AI outputs must state what materials were used, what is missing, which content is observation/inference/analogy, who is responsible for the final judgment, how it may be used, and when it is to be reviewed or withdrawn. Where disclosure of identity, retaliation risk, or justified opacity is involved, protective omissions must remain distinguishable from “no evidence.”</p><p>Responsibility for AI use should also reflect the actual division of work among those who provide materials, decide objectives and permissions, maintain systems, carry out actions, and review results. Facilities and procedures that perform technical steps do not become subjects capable of bearing moral or legal responsibility merely because they output recommendations or execute instructions. Organizations legally capable of bearing a specified responsibility must be assessed separately according to their institutional status and the matter at issue. Greater automation does not put all responsibility on the person who clicks last. Reviewers must have sufficient materials, time, capability, and authority to stop actions in practice. The organization must also provide the resources needed for these conditions.</p></div>',1)),n(i,{section:"13.8"}),e[9]||(e[9]=t('<div class="theory-source"><h2 id="section-13-9" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">13.9</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Run Records and Failure Semantics <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-13-9" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;13.9 Run Records and Failure Semantics {#section-13-9}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Each tool run creates a <code>flow_execution_record</code> with a closed set of fields. It registers object and scale records, the L1–L3 level, IT1–IT4 results, IG1–IG7 results, whether the five gates and 13 steps were run, whether SJ1–SJ8 were triggered, evidence, alternative explanations, uncertainty, output categories, judgment and action ceilings, normative handoff, appeal, rollback, and status.</p><p>The action ceiling uses a closed lattice of output ceilings: <code>description_only</code>, <code>diagnostic_only</code>, and <code>requirements_only</code>. Each level has fixed permitted output categories and has <code>can_authorize=false</code> and <code>can_execute=false</code>. The caller must not claim “already authorized” in free text or expand the permitted categories on its own. <code>action_requirements</code> only specifies conditions for the external choice and authorization procedures. It is not an executable command.</p><p>A normative handoff must link to verifiable external choice, C12, and authorization records, or state explicitly that these are missing. References must specify the object, action, scope, time, and actual grounds for verification. Vague labels, self-reports of having passed, or circular references to the same record cannot establish permission. The implementation determines the reference format. The tool is still prohibited from issuing its own authorization.</p><p>Outcome statuses must remain distinct. <code>passed</code> means the recording requirements at that gate were met. <code>failed</code> means the step ran and failed. <code>not_run</code> means it was not run because of an earlier gate or a scope restriction. <code>unknown</code>, <code>not_applicable</code>, <code>not_observable</code>, and <code>withheld_for_protection</code> remain four distinct missing-data statuses. Failure must not be rewritten as inapplicability. Concealment for protection must not be rewritten as absence.</p><p>The run record’s <code>authorization_effect</code> is always <code>none</code>. Even when external references for normative choice, C12, J, and O exist, this record only checks them and hands them off. It does not issue authorization. All reviewed outputs must retain an accessible appeal and a means of actual withdrawal or correction. A tool conclusion that cannot be withdrawn must not be put to a high-impact use that is difficult to reverse.</p></div>',1)),n(i,{section:"13.9"}),e[10]||(e[10]=t('<div class="theory-source"><h2 id="section-13-10" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">13.10</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>The Nine Gates for Multi-Circle Projection <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-13-10" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;13.10 The Nine Gates for Multi-Circle Projection {#section-13-10}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The seven gates are the only main workflow for explanation and diagnosis. When a task explicitly calls for dynamic projection, conditional foresight, or bounded choice, elaborate that workflow with the nine gates. The nine gates must not bypass the seven gates’ requirements for the object, evidence, affected positions, power, reflexivity, the strong-judgment procedure, and the action ceiling.</p><div class="xk-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Gate</th><th>Core Check</th><th>Output on Failure</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>DF1 Freeze the Facts</td><td>Event type, source, time, evidence cutoff, and disputes</td><td>List of factual questions</td></tr><tr><td>DF2 Joint Object</td><td>Actors, circles, relations, membership, and K</td><td>Candidate groupings or a return to a single focus</td></tr><tr><td>DF3 Two Channels and Clocks</td><td>Material and experience–meaning conditions relevant to the task, clocks, and unknowns. Mark irrelevant items inapplicable with a reason</td><td>Retain the state snapshot. Suspend propagation that depends on missing conditions and review independent paths separately</td></tr><tr><td>DF4 Propagation Through Mechanisms</td><td>Channels, thresholds, delay, feedback, and cascades</td><td>Candidate mechanisms and observations that can distinguish them</td></tr><tr><td>DF5 Branching Paths</td><td>Conditions, parent and child nodes, and early and contrary signals</td><td>Parallel paths without forced ordering</td></tr><tr><td>DF6 Forecast Registration</td><td>Target, horizon, simple baseline, calibration, and write-back</td><td>Scenario projection only</td></tr><tr><td>DF7 Normative Choice</td><td>N, PF, affected people, options, and no action</td><td>Suspend recommendations blocked by the gap. Retain evaluations that stand independently, conditional recommendations under explicit premises, and requirements for more evidence. This confers no permission to execute</td></tr><tr><td>DF8 Authorization and Execution</td><td>J, O, responsibility, stopping, rollback, and remedy</td><td>External handoff without execution</td></tr><tr><td>DF9 Outcome Write-Back</td><td>Results, comparison with the baseline, deviations, downgrades, and retirement</td><td>Retain the failure and open a new version</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The tool can propose candidate circles at DF2 and variable candidates at DF5. Every candidate retains the status <code>candidate</code>. If DF6 does not pass, outputs must not use forecast language such as “will happen” or “expected to prove accurate.” If DF7 or DF8 does not pass, outputs must not give executable instructions. DF9 does not permit overwriting the original run.</p></div>',1)),n(i,{section:"13.10"}),e[11]||(e[11]=t('<div class="theory-source"><h2 id="section-13-11" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">13.11</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Limits on AI in Dynamic Runs <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-13-11" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;13.11 Limits on AI in Dynamic Runs {#section-13-11}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>AI can organize materials, check original passages that can be located, find gaps in fields, generate and compare competing paths, check contract consistency, and calculate registered metrics. Under explicit normative premises it can also form well-grounded local judgments and recommendations and draft complete texts. AI cannot certify a real-world event solely by its own generation, self-report, or internal procedure. It cannot diagnose personality, disclose information on behalf of those concerned, impose circle boundaries on others through its own classifications, treat simulations as facts, settle disputed value premises for others, or issue authorization. Whether external material supports a particular fact must be judged separately according to the material’s status, method, counterexamples, and intended use. Faithfulness to a source does not automatically establish that what it says about the world is true. For a variable automatically proposed by AI, the system must record its status as a model candidate, the minimal test, and prohibited uses. It must not be upgraded before the corresponding external evidence is available.</p></div>',1)),n(i,{section:"13.11"}),e[12]||(e[12]=t('<div class="theory-source"><h3 id="section-13-11-1" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">13.11.1</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Checking AI Materials and the Chain of Tool Actions <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-13-11-1" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;13.11.1 Checking AI Materials and the Chain of Tool Actions {#section-13-11-1}&quot;">​</a></h3><p>When checking a generated answer, examine separately whether the source exists, whether the citation details are correct, whether the original passage supports the sentence, and whether the fact asserted in the sentence has adequate grounds. A source’s silence on the matter does not establish that the sentence is wrong. Accurately restating a mistaken source does not establish the facts. Where local knowledge, oral accounts, or protected materials have no public webpage, retain appropriate means of verification and limits on public disclosure. They must not be treated as nonexistent on that basis alone.</p><p>Retrieval augmentation can improve access to materials and performance on a task. Consistency or uncertainty checks can also detect certain errors. Their effectiveness remains limited by retrieval coverage, shared sources, and the types of error. An error repeated reliably can appear consistent. Several models repeating the same inaccurate material do not supply several independent pieces of evidence. A formal checker can confirm a derivation under stated premises, but cannot establish that those premises hold in reality.</p><p>For automated action, record the recommendation, tool request, application execution, external state, and consequences separately. The model writing “stopped” does not establish that the executor received a stop instruction. An interface showing “deleted” does not establish that downstream copies are gone. A person changing a recommendation does not establish that the change took effect before an irreversible action. Permissions for actual protection and correction must be exercisable in the corresponding system.</p></div>',1)),n(i,{section:"13.11.1"}),e[13]||(e[13]=t('<div class="theory-source"><h3 id="section-13-11-2" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">13.11.2</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Task Endpoints and a Verifiable Execution Chain <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-13-11-2" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;13.11.2 Task Endpoints and a Verifiable Execution Chain {#section-13-11-2}&quot;">​</a></h3><p>The endpoint for verifying a tool chain is determined by the user’s request or the institutional task. If the request is only for a draft, delivery can be complete when the draft has been saved correctly, can be opened, and its content meets the requirements. If the task requires changing an external record, success returned by the tool must still be confirmed by the state of the corresponding external object. If the task requires actual remedy, verify that the resources, rights, or services reach those concerned. Actual completion of a narrower endpoint need not wait for every consequence in people’s lives. A success message in the interface cannot substitute for completion of a broader endpoint.</p><div class="xk-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Stage</th><th>Minimum Object of Verification</th><th>Typical Break</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Form the recommendation</td><td>Objective, materials, object to be changed, and permitted scope</td><td>Treating generated text as already adopted</td></tr><tr><td>Make the tool request</td><td>Specific action, parameters, current object version, and permissions</td><td>Sending a correct recommendation to the wrong object</td></tr><tr><td>Execute in the application</td><td>Whether the request was received, executed, or rejected, and how repeated requests are handled</td><td>A request arrives but is queued, fails, or is executed repeatedly</td></tr><tr><td>Check external state</td><td>Whether the target and dependent objects changed as intended</td><td>A local marker changes while actual execution still reads the old state</td></tr><tr><td>Check the task outcome</td><td>The specified benefit or protection endpoint and remaining problems</td><td>A corrected record takes effect, but the missed opportunity is not restored</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>For concurrent operations across tools, first distinguish operations that can read independently, operations that can modify independently, and operations that write to the same object. Changes to shared state must preserve ordering, version prerequisites, or conflict handling. Before an irreversible action, verify again that permissions are still valid and check the stopping conditions. A request timing out means its outcome is unknown. Do not assume it was not executed and repeat it. Check the request identifier or object state first, then decide whether to retry. These are responsibilities for execution. They do not require every application to use the same log format.</p><p>Stopping has its own completion conditions. Record separately the issuing of a stop instruction, acknowledgment by the executor, termination of actions already in progress, and remedy of consequences that have already occurred. If only the first two steps are confirmed, report that scope accurately. If winding down safely requires a limited action under a separate responsibility, continue it on its own grounds. Stopping the main task must not mean abandoning the necessary continuation. When repair fails, keep a record of the object for which repair failed and of the real-world consequences. A new, normal output must not overwrite them.</p></div>',1)),n(i,{section:"13.11.2"}),e[14]||(e[14]=t('<div class="theory-source"></div><hr class="footnotes-sep"><section class="footnotes"><ol class="footnotes-list"><li id="fn1" class="footnote-item"><p>Translator&#39;s note: Diagnosis here denotes a nonclinical assessment within the framework. <a href="#fnref1" class="footnote-backref">↩︎</a></p></li><li id="fn2" class="footnote-item"><p>Translator&#39;s note: A contract here is an analytical agreement registered and frozen before results, akin to pre-registration. <a href="#fnref2" class="footnote-backref">↩︎</a></p></li></ol></section>',3))])}const b=a(c,[["render",d]]);export{g as __pageData,b as default};
